00:03:56.880 --> 00:03:59.860
and the details of various
kinds of easy to locate.

00:03:59.860 --> 00:04:02.655
So when can open up a
journal in microbiology

00:04:02.655 --> 00:04:05.150
and be assailed with detailed models

00:04:05.150 --> 00:04:07.730
of the causal structure
of cells and proteins.

00:04:07.730 --> 00:04:11.810
One can textbooks on the
increasing array of computational

00:04:11.810 --> 00:04:14.561
tools for causal search and discovery

00:04:14.561 --> 00:04:18.360
and take classes devoted to,
for example, formal methods

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:19.644
and causal modeling.

00:04:19.644 --> 00:04:22.449
Psychologists are unraveling new details

00:04:22.449 --> 00:04:26.894
about how people use causal
concepts and reasoning

00:04:26.894 --> 00:04:31.320
and will details of these
kinds are in abundant supply.

00:04:31.320 --> 00:04:33.553
It's not easy to find among them

00:04:33.553 --> 00:04:37.420
and answer to the question
of what causation is

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.160
or how it fits into

00:04:41.700 --> 00:04:43.900
a physics-based ontology.

00:04:43.900 --> 00:04:45.540
So I'm gonna try to fill that hole

00:04:45.540 --> 00:04:49.630
by assembling the pieces
of our understanding,

00:04:49.630 --> 00:04:52.040
emerging scientific
understanding causation

00:04:52.040 --> 00:04:55.920
into an account of where
and how causation arises

00:04:55.920 --> 00:04:59.040
in the architecture of the physical world

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.710
with special attention to
the contested questions

00:05:01.710 --> 00:05:04.893
of where the direction
of causation comes from

00:05:04.893 --> 00:05:08.440
it's connection to the
second law of thermodynamics

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:11.890
and the relationship between
causation and agency.

00:05:11.890 --> 00:05:14.287
So if you've been attending
other talks in the series,

00:05:14.287 --> 00:05:17.390
you'll have heard about
some of these things.

00:05:17.390 --> 00:05:21.150
What I say will have the most in common

00:05:21.150 --> 00:05:23.730
with Carlo Rovelli's beautiful talk

00:05:23.730 --> 00:05:25.440
which opened the series,

00:05:25.440 --> 00:05:27.750
with maybe a little more focused attention

00:05:27.750 --> 00:05:29.840
on the philosophical questions.

00:05:29.840 --> 00:05:32.733
So this will sort of bring
things background full circle.

00:05:33.770 --> 00:05:36.530
I'm gonna adopt a very
high level perspective,

00:05:36.530 --> 00:05:39.553
kind of pulling our gaze away from details

00:05:39.553 --> 00:05:42.500
to try to pull the big pieces into place,

00:05:42.500 --> 00:05:44.863
to get an overall view of the whole Vista.

00:05:46.030 --> 00:05:51.030
So if one looks back at the
early history of science,

00:05:52.200 --> 00:05:55.369
it can be seen as developing
out of systematization

00:05:55.369 --> 00:05:59.090
and abstraction of causal thinking.

00:05:59.090 --> 00:06:02.240
The search for an understanding
of the causal relations

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:05.400
among events that is as true physics

00:06:05.400 --> 00:06:06.970
as it is at the other sciences.

00:06:06.970 --> 00:06:10.270
So I think it's not wrong
to say as Russell Bertrand,

00:06:10.270 --> 00:06:12.360
Russell did in 1955,

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:16.009
that the aim of physics
consciously or unconsciously

00:06:16.009 --> 00:06:18.960
has always been to
discover what we may call

00:06:18.960 --> 00:06:21.823
the causal skeleton of the world.

00:06:23.460 --> 00:06:24.293
Now at first,

00:06:24.293 --> 00:06:27.040
the notion of cause retained
its close connection

00:06:27.040 --> 00:06:28.330
with mechanical ideas.

00:06:28.330 --> 00:06:31.305
So a cause was something
that brought about its effect

00:06:31.305 --> 00:06:34.509
by a transfer of force.

00:06:34.509 --> 00:06:37.610
But by the time Newton
had finished with it,

00:06:37.610 --> 00:06:40.900
causation didn't appear
in the final presentation

00:06:40.900 --> 00:06:43.110
of his own theory at all.

00:06:43.110 --> 00:06:45.457
What he put in the place
of causal relations

00:06:45.457 --> 00:06:48.403
are mathematical equations, oops sorry.

00:06:50.110 --> 00:06:51.920
Mathematical equations,

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.063
usually express in the form
of differential equations,

00:06:55.063 --> 00:06:58.862
like give the rate of change
of a quantity over time.

00:06:58.862 --> 00:07:01.835
Causes and the associated idea of force

00:07:01.835 --> 00:07:06.270
dropped out of the final
presentation entirely.

00:07:06.270 --> 00:07:08.700
There's no direction
and there's no asymmetry

00:07:08.700 --> 00:07:10.062
of determination.

00:07:10.062 --> 00:07:12.820
Later events determined earlier ones

00:07:12.820 --> 00:07:16.115
as surely as earlier ones,
determined later ones.

00:07:16.115 --> 00:07:20.720
And it's clear from the quote
that I just said about earlier

00:07:20.720 --> 00:07:21.820
from Russell,

00:07:21.820 --> 00:07:23.650
that Russell actually came around

00:07:23.650 --> 00:07:25.620
to thinking quite differently.

00:07:25.620 --> 00:07:27.130
But 32 years earlier,

00:07:27.130 --> 00:07:30.250
he wrote in a paper on the notion of cause

00:07:30.250 --> 00:07:32.132
that's the starting point of all,

00:07:32.132 --> 00:07:36.513
almost every discussion
of causation and physics.

00:07:37.381 --> 00:07:39.260
Now in that paper,

00:07:39.260 --> 00:07:41.566
Russell noted that the kinds of laws

00:07:41.566 --> 00:07:43.760
that Newtonian mechanic gives us

00:07:43.760 --> 00:07:45.820
are so different from causal relations

00:07:45.820 --> 00:07:47.720
as traditionally conceived.

00:07:47.720 --> 00:07:50.870
Thus misleading to think of
them in causal terms at all.

00:07:50.870 --> 00:07:54.530
And he urged that, sorry,
I keep doing this, sorry.

00:07:54.530 --> 00:07:59.280
He urged that causation is a
folk notion that has no place

00:07:59.280 --> 00:08:00.523
in immature science,

00:08:01.370 --> 00:08:03.186
the modern discussion of causation

00:08:03.186 --> 00:08:05.720
and the philosophy of science really began

00:08:05.720 --> 00:08:09.950
after Russell with Cartwright's
deeply influential critique

00:08:09.950 --> 00:08:11.578
of Russell's paper.

00:08:11.578 --> 00:08:14.877
And what Cartwright pointed
out was that dynamic laws

00:08:14.877 --> 00:08:17.854
cant play the role of
causal relations in science

00:08:17.854 --> 00:08:20.996
because specifically causal
information is needed

00:08:20.996 --> 00:08:24.501
to distinguish effective
from ineffective strategies

00:08:24.501 --> 00:08:27.040
for bringing about ends.

00:08:27.040 --> 00:08:27.920
So for example,

00:08:27.920 --> 00:08:30.271
it might be true as a matter of law

00:08:30.271 --> 00:08:33.310
because smoking causes bad breath,

00:08:33.310 --> 00:08:35.560
but there's a strong, positive correlation

00:08:35.560 --> 00:08:37.940
between bad breath and cancer.

00:08:37.940 --> 00:08:41.120
But it is not true that
bad breath causes cancer.

00:08:41.120 --> 00:08:44.090
And hence it's not true
that treating bad breath

00:08:44.090 --> 00:08:47.340
is my effective strategy
for preventing cancer.

00:08:47.340 --> 00:08:48.280
And that difference,

00:08:48.280 --> 00:08:51.354
the difference between
being correlated with cancer

00:08:51.354 --> 00:08:53.950
and being a way of bringing it about

00:08:53.950 --> 00:08:55.590
is not one that can captured

00:08:55.590 --> 00:08:58.550
by looking at only at the dynamical loss.

00:08:58.550 --> 00:09:00.630
So if one wants to avoid getting cancer

00:09:00.630 --> 00:09:04.170
or one has to know not simply
what cancer is correlated with

00:09:04.170 --> 00:09:07.240
as a matter of physical
law, but what causes it,

00:09:07.240 --> 00:09:09.900
that is what brings it about.

00:09:09.900 --> 00:09:11.596
So this was Cartwright's observation

00:09:11.596 --> 00:09:14.890
and it led to a lot of
hand-wringing wondering

00:09:14.890 --> 00:09:17.190
what causal information
adds to the kind of

00:09:17.190 --> 00:09:19.680
global dynamic laws that Russell took

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:21.792
as paradigmatic, the physical laws,

00:09:21.792 --> 00:09:24.690
and philosophers played
around a little bit

00:09:24.690 --> 00:09:28.540
with probabilistic and
counterfactual analysis.

00:09:28.540 --> 00:09:30.600
And there were a lot
of unresolved questions

00:09:30.600 --> 00:09:33.153
about the metaphysics of causal relations.

00:09:33.153 --> 00:09:35.968
One strand in contemporary discussion,

00:09:35.968 --> 00:09:39.930
followed Russel in denying
that physics has any real place

00:09:39.930 --> 00:09:42.960
for causation and continued
to reject causal talk

00:09:42.960 --> 00:09:45.950
as anything but folk physics.

00:09:45.950 --> 00:09:48.880
Now in the last 15 years or so.

00:09:48.880 --> 00:09:51.130
However, there's been a real revolution

00:09:51.130 --> 00:09:53.189
in our understanding of causal thinking

00:09:53.189 --> 00:09:55.478
stemming from a lot ally developments

00:09:55.478 --> 00:09:57.180
in a coalition of fields

00:09:57.180 --> 00:10:00.890
from computer science to psychology.

00:10:00.890 --> 00:10:04.319
And it was really the structural
causal modeling framework

00:10:04.319 --> 00:10:08.250
from Pearl and sort of
parallel development

00:10:08.250 --> 00:10:10.720
since Beartooth be more in shyness.

00:10:10.720 --> 00:10:13.246
And its philosophical
development by Jim Woodward

00:10:13.246 --> 00:10:15.506
that transformed the discussion.

00:10:15.506 --> 00:10:17.660
So what the framework does

00:10:17.660 --> 00:10:20.900
is it gives us a precise, formal framework

00:10:20.900 --> 00:10:25.165
for representing causal relationships.

00:10:25.165 --> 00:10:30.165
That's well-suited to causal
search and discovery in science

00:10:30.230 --> 00:10:33.893
has been at the center of
developments in causal learning

00:10:33.893 --> 00:10:35.450
and statistical inference.

00:10:35.450 --> 00:10:38.300
It can be used to define
normative solutions,

00:10:38.300 --> 00:10:40.600
to causal inference and judgment problems.

00:10:40.600 --> 00:10:42.320
It's facilitated new insights

00:10:42.320 --> 00:10:44.761
into rural causal thinking cognition,

00:10:44.761 --> 00:10:49.700
the influence and the power of
that framework are undisputed

00:10:49.700 --> 00:10:52.150
and largely due to its utility

00:10:52.150 --> 00:10:54.719
in raising concrete scientific questions.

00:10:54.719 --> 00:10:58.614
That can be investigated in laboratory.

00:10:58.614 --> 00:11:00.636
From a philosophical point of view,

00:11:00.636 --> 00:11:03.960
what it's done is helped
us resolve I think,

00:11:03.960 --> 00:11:05.865
the puzzle brought out by the exchange

00:11:05.865 --> 00:11:07.970
between Russell and Cartwright.

00:11:07.970 --> 00:11:11.720
That is the disappearance of
causes from the fundamental

00:11:11.720 --> 00:11:15.350
level of a physical
description on the one hand

00:11:15.350 --> 00:11:18.300
and their indispensability
and practical reasoning

00:11:18.300 --> 00:11:19.740
on the other.

00:11:19.740 --> 00:11:21.860
And the way that it resolves the puzzle

00:11:21.860 --> 00:11:24.506
is by giving us an
articulated understanding

00:11:24.506 --> 00:11:29.352
of where and how causation
or better causal thinking

00:11:29.352 --> 00:11:32.044
and the structures that
support causal thinking

00:11:32.044 --> 00:11:35.270
arise in the architecture of the cosmos.

00:11:35.270 --> 00:11:37.950
And in the way that is characteristic

00:11:37.950 --> 00:11:39.940
of philosophical questions once they pass

00:11:39.940 --> 00:11:41.440
into the hands of the sciences

00:11:41.440 --> 00:11:43.925
is transformed some of the old questions

00:11:43.925 --> 00:11:45.913
while also raising new ones.

00:11:47.059 --> 00:11:50.610
So just to highlight a
few relevant features

00:11:50.610 --> 00:11:51.580
of the formalism.

00:11:51.580 --> 00:11:53.710
So type causation is basic.

00:11:53.710 --> 00:11:57.280
That is causation between types of events

00:11:57.280 --> 00:11:59.240
and it's relative to networks.

00:11:59.240 --> 00:12:01.950
So type causation relates types of events

00:12:01.950 --> 00:12:05.170
like the striking of a
match of a specified kind

00:12:05.170 --> 00:12:07.438
under given circumstances

00:12:07.438 --> 00:12:09.525
rather than the particular striking

00:12:09.525 --> 00:12:13.940
of a particular match at a
particular time and place.

00:12:13.940 --> 00:12:15.960
And this accords well with science,

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:18.450
'cause with practice in science,

00:12:18.450 --> 00:12:20.670
because scientists to a large part

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:25.296
devoted to the identification
of type causal relationships

00:12:25.296 --> 00:12:28.620
networks are defined by
collections of variables,

00:12:28.620 --> 00:12:31.500
individual properties are
represented by the values

00:12:31.500 --> 00:12:32.840
of variables,

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:34.935
the choice of which variables to include

00:12:34.935 --> 00:12:37.890
and which to leave out of a network

00:12:37.890 --> 00:12:39.700
when studying a causal claim,

00:12:39.700 --> 00:12:42.120
make a difference to whether
there is a causal link

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:45.898
between the pair of variables
and what the link is.

00:12:45.898 --> 00:12:48.060
Singular causal claims.

00:12:48.060 --> 00:12:51.230
So like the claim that a
dot on a photographic plate

00:12:51.230 --> 00:12:54.193
was caused by the impact of an electron.

00:12:54.193 --> 00:12:57.383
Those derived from type causation.

00:12:57.383 --> 00:12:59.930
DAGs this is just again,

00:12:59.930 --> 00:13:03.072
introducing relevant
features in the formalism

00:13:03.072 --> 00:13:05.300
DAGs directed acyclic graphs

00:13:05.300 --> 00:13:07.370
are used to represent causal relations

00:13:07.370 --> 00:13:09.200
among variables and network.

00:13:09.200 --> 00:13:12.493
So direct causation is
represented by an arrow,

00:13:16.250 --> 00:13:19.780
and it's the most basic
causal relation in a network.

00:13:19.780 --> 00:13:22.440
A variable Xi for example,

00:13:22.440 --> 00:13:25.350
is a direct cause of another variable Xj

00:13:25.350 --> 00:13:27.433
relative to a set of variables V,

00:13:28.688 --> 00:13:32.289
just in case there is an
intervention on the first

00:13:32.289 --> 00:13:35.080
that will change the value of the second

00:13:35.080 --> 00:13:37.390
or change your probability distribution

00:13:37.390 --> 00:13:39.070
over values of the second.

00:13:39.070 --> 00:13:41.700
When all of the variables
in the networking question,

00:13:41.700 --> 00:13:44.970
except those two are held fixed.

00:13:44.970 --> 00:13:47.120
So DAGs these diagrams

00:13:47.120 --> 00:13:49.020
give us a conspicuous way of displaying

00:13:49.020 --> 00:13:50.413
causal relationships.

00:13:52.440 --> 00:13:53.450
An example like this,

00:13:53.450 --> 00:13:56.092
you can see immediately how intervening

00:13:56.092 --> 00:13:59.933
on one variable will affect
other variables in the network.

00:14:01.130 --> 00:14:04.020
Now, from the point of
view of this framework,

00:14:04.020 --> 00:14:06.270
causal judgements in everyday contexts

00:14:06.270 --> 00:14:09.086
rely on loose and tacit assumptions

00:14:09.086 --> 00:14:11.480
about which variables
are being considered.

00:14:11.480 --> 00:14:13.370
What's being held fixed in situ

00:14:13.370 --> 00:14:15.090
for the purposes of assessing

00:14:15.090 --> 00:14:17.270
the effects of one variable on another.

00:14:17.270 --> 00:14:21.290
Those choices are made
explicit in the formalization.

00:14:21.290 --> 00:14:23.370
And because it makes them explicit,

00:14:23.370 --> 00:14:26.635
it lets us separate the objective
content of causal claims

00:14:26.635 --> 00:14:30.183
from the sort of loose context,
dependent, pragmatic factors

00:14:30.183 --> 00:14:32.393
that guide the choice of the network.

00:14:33.940 --> 00:14:36.410
So the question that comes out of

00:14:36.410 --> 00:14:38.260
the Russell Cartwright exchange

00:14:38.260 --> 00:14:41.495
was how does causal information

00:14:41.495 --> 00:14:46.380
recall go beyond the information
captured in dynamical loss

00:14:46.380 --> 00:14:50.530
that is, what does causal
information add to those laws?

00:14:50.530 --> 00:14:52.380
Or if it add anything,

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:54.999
what information contained in those laws

00:14:54.999 --> 00:14:57.192
doesn't make it explicit?

00:14:57.192 --> 00:15:01.238
So here's the way to think
of it, global dynamic laws,

00:15:01.238 --> 00:15:04.570
the kinds of laws that
Newton's theory gave us.

00:15:04.570 --> 00:15:07.780
Give us information about
the relationships between

00:15:07.780 --> 00:15:10.120
global states at different times.

00:15:10.120 --> 00:15:13.650
Causal information, is
information about the results

00:15:13.650 --> 00:15:15.583
of the hypothetical interventions,

00:15:15.583 --> 00:15:20.010
where by that we mean, questions
about what would happen.

00:15:20.010 --> 00:15:23.780
If a variable were separated
from its earlier history

00:15:23.780 --> 00:15:25.403
and allowed to vary freely.

00:15:26.540 --> 00:15:30.660
Now the difficulty of with
extracting causal information

00:15:30.660 --> 00:15:32.550
from global dynamic laws,

00:15:32.550 --> 00:15:34.120
is that the global dynamic laws

00:15:34.120 --> 00:15:36.970
don't allow the separation of variable

00:15:36.970 --> 00:15:39.220
from its own past causes.

00:15:39.220 --> 00:15:40.180
So here's an example.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:43.380
Suppose someone presents you
with something like this.

00:15:43.380 --> 00:15:47.250
The schematized model
of an engine for a car.

00:15:47.250 --> 00:15:49.924
Has a collection of components,

00:15:49.924 --> 00:15:53.106
a piston, an engine block, a timing chain,

00:15:53.106 --> 00:15:56.100
a crank shaft, and so on.

00:15:56.100 --> 00:15:57.625
Now the parts move,

00:15:57.625 --> 00:16:00.906
but so long as they're assembled
in the form of an engine,

00:16:00.906 --> 00:16:03.732
they have a restricted range of motion

00:16:03.732 --> 00:16:05.810
relative to one another.

00:16:05.810 --> 00:16:07.496
So if we modeled the engine,

00:16:07.496 --> 00:16:11.460
remodeled the engine at
the level of description

00:16:11.460 --> 00:16:14.323
that treats these functional
components as basic parts,

00:16:14.323 --> 00:16:16.997
we can come up with dynamical equations,

00:16:16.997 --> 00:16:20.244
that would describe how the
global state of the engine

00:16:20.244 --> 00:16:21.849
varies over time.

00:16:21.849 --> 00:16:25.387
And those dynamical
equations for the engine

00:16:25.387 --> 00:16:27.640
would tell you that there's
a fixed relationship

00:16:27.640 --> 00:16:31.421
with for example, the
pistons and the valve.

00:16:31.421 --> 00:16:33.760
So given the equations,

00:16:33.760 --> 00:16:36.143
you could use the position
of one of those things

00:16:36.143 --> 00:16:38.450
to calculate the position of the other.

00:16:38.450 --> 00:16:41.047
So maybe when one is up the other's down,

00:16:41.047 --> 00:16:43.356
when the other is down,
the other one is up,

00:16:43.356 --> 00:16:46.555
they move, but they move together.

00:16:46.555 --> 00:16:50.669
And if you just look at the equations,

00:16:50.669 --> 00:16:53.920
there would be three causal
hypotheses that you might

00:16:53.920 --> 00:16:55.223
come up with.

00:16:55.223 --> 00:16:59.074
One is that the position of
A causes the position of B,

00:16:59.074 --> 00:17:02.660
that the position of B
causes the position of A,

00:17:02.660 --> 00:17:04.790
or that they have a common cause.

00:17:04.790 --> 00:17:07.290
Now remember the global
dynamical equations

00:17:07.290 --> 00:17:11.414
just fixed the relative values of A and B.

00:17:11.414 --> 00:17:14.067
These three hypotheses, however,

00:17:14.067 --> 00:17:16.562
have different modal implications.

00:17:16.562 --> 00:17:18.210
So if A causes B,

00:17:18.210 --> 00:17:22.896
then if you kept everything
else fixed and you reached in

00:17:22.896 --> 00:17:25.008
and you moved A up and down,

00:17:25.008 --> 00:17:27.960
you should be able to see B move as well.

00:17:27.960 --> 00:17:30.720
So that's what you would be committed to,

00:17:30.720 --> 00:17:33.690
if you asserted that A causes B.

00:17:33.690 --> 00:17:36.860
If B causes A, then the other thing holds,

00:17:36.860 --> 00:17:38.856
you should be able to reach in

00:17:38.856 --> 00:17:43.856
and move B keeping everything
else fixed and A should move.

00:17:44.370 --> 00:17:46.210
If the two have a common cause

00:17:46.210 --> 00:17:49.195
then neither of these
interventions should affect

00:17:49.195 --> 00:17:53.740
the value of the other,
since the common cause C

00:17:53.740 --> 00:17:55.010
would be left unaffected,

00:17:55.010 --> 00:17:56.810
and it's the thing that causes both.

00:17:58.017 --> 00:18:01.970
Now, in the case of an open
system, like a car engine

00:18:01.970 --> 00:18:05.410
intervention from the outside
are physically realizable

00:18:05.410 --> 00:18:08.720
and often in a way that
serves to effectively separate

00:18:08.720 --> 00:18:11.100
the variable from its own past causes

00:18:11.100 --> 00:18:13.065
inside the system of interest.

00:18:13.065 --> 00:18:15.361
I'll say a little bit
more about this below,

00:18:15.361 --> 00:18:19.290
but what the example here does
is it makes a logical point

00:18:20.582 --> 00:18:25.098
that the dynamical equations
for the engine as a whole,

00:18:25.098 --> 00:18:28.067
don't tell you which of
these hypotheses are correct,

00:18:28.067 --> 00:18:31.060
because the counterfactuals that you need

00:18:31.060 --> 00:18:33.050
to discriminate between these hypotheses,

00:18:33.050 --> 00:18:37.283
between the three causal hypotheses here,

00:18:38.210 --> 00:18:40.700
namely, what would happen

00:18:40.700 --> 00:18:42.640
if we could hold everything else fixed

00:18:42.640 --> 00:18:45.870
and intervene from the
outside to alter the variable

00:18:45.870 --> 00:18:47.977
of one of the values in the network.

00:18:47.977 --> 00:18:51.630
Those counterfactuals are
physically impossible.

00:18:51.630 --> 00:18:54.440
There is no model of the engine as a whole

00:18:54.440 --> 00:18:56.523
compatible with this global dynamic laws,

00:18:56.523 --> 00:19:00.302
in which the antecedents
of those counterfactuals

00:19:00.302 --> 00:19:02.170
is realized.

00:19:02.170 --> 00:19:06.370
So if the engine comprised
the universe as a whole

00:19:06.370 --> 00:19:08.685
and the global equations
were all that we had,

00:19:08.685 --> 00:19:11.580
then there would be no way of discerning

00:19:11.580 --> 00:19:14.607
the underlying causal
structure of the engine,

00:19:14.607 --> 00:19:17.673
just by looking at the dynamical laws.

00:19:21.244 --> 00:19:23.327
The reason that, that is,

00:19:25.540 --> 00:19:27.597
is not that the information
that we can extract

00:19:27.597 --> 00:19:29.033
from global laws,

00:19:29.033 --> 00:19:32.860
it's not that laws don't
have modal content,

00:19:32.860 --> 00:19:36.870
is that the modal content
that they have is global.

00:19:36.870 --> 00:19:39.500
So global laws will tell
us what kinds of worlds

00:19:39.500 --> 00:19:43.080
are possible, but they don't
tell us what would happen

00:19:43.080 --> 00:19:46.470
if we could intervene
on one of those worlds

00:19:46.470 --> 00:19:48.454
in a way that's contrary to law.

00:19:48.454 --> 00:19:50.173
So in logical terms,

00:19:50.173 --> 00:19:53.980
we would say the antecedents
to intervention counterfactuals

00:19:53.980 --> 00:19:56.430
are normal logically impossible.

00:19:56.430 --> 00:19:58.580
That is they're contrary to law.

00:19:58.580 --> 00:20:00.820
So if we take the global
laws is more basic,

00:20:00.820 --> 00:20:05.613
we don't generally get causal information.

00:20:05.613 --> 00:20:08.500
So that tells us something about

00:20:08.500 --> 00:20:10.560
in terms of the content of causal claims,

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.510
what distinguishes them

00:20:12.510 --> 00:20:15.360
from what we can get out
of global dynamical laws.

00:20:15.360 --> 00:20:17.797
But it gives rise to another question.

00:20:17.797 --> 00:20:19.210
And the question is,

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:24.066
how is causal knowledge
possible at our world?

00:20:24.066 --> 00:20:28.160
If causal information is
information about what would happen

00:20:28.160 --> 00:20:30.838
if some variable were
separated from its past causes

00:20:30.838 --> 00:20:32.969
and allowed to vary freely.

00:20:32.969 --> 00:20:33.897
And in fact,

00:20:33.897 --> 00:20:38.078
no variable is ever actually
separated from its past causes.

00:20:38.078 --> 00:20:41.210
How could we study causal structure?

00:20:41.210 --> 00:20:44.787
And the answer is that in practical terms,

00:20:44.787 --> 00:20:49.070
there are various ways
effectively randomizing

00:20:49.070 --> 00:20:50.331
the values of the variable,

00:20:50.331 --> 00:20:53.450
which provides a separation
and effective separation

00:20:53.450 --> 00:20:57.480
from its own past causes so
that we can treat changes

00:20:57.480 --> 00:21:00.352
to the values of that
variable as interventions.

00:21:00.352 --> 00:21:02.493
So for example, flip a coin,

00:21:02.493 --> 00:21:05.959
have a random number
generator set the value for

00:21:05.959 --> 00:21:08.850
the variable you want to intervene on,

00:21:08.850 --> 00:21:10.757
let a lab assistant choose one at-will.

00:21:10.757 --> 00:21:14.408
Any variable that can be manipulated

00:21:14.408 --> 00:21:17.310
and whose own causes are uncorrelated

00:21:17.310 --> 00:21:19.410
with the variables of interest.

00:21:19.410 --> 00:21:24.410
That is the variables on which

00:21:24.451 --> 00:21:26.590
the causal impact of the variable

00:21:26.590 --> 00:21:27.937
that you're intervening on

00:21:27.937 --> 00:21:29.996
is the one that you're interested in

00:21:29.996 --> 00:21:32.011
can be regarded for such purposes

00:21:32.011 --> 00:21:34.273
as external to the system

00:21:34.273 --> 00:21:37.160
and changes in the value of such variables

00:21:37.160 --> 00:21:39.158
can be regarded as interventions.

00:21:39.158 --> 00:21:44.158
This of course is the essence
of scientific experimentation,

00:21:44.267 --> 00:21:48.080
the possibility of knowledge
of causal knowledge

00:21:48.080 --> 00:21:50.851
rests on the fact that we can effectively

00:21:50.851 --> 00:21:53.200
isolate systems in the laboratory,

00:21:53.200 --> 00:21:55.575
manipulate the external input to them

00:21:55.575 --> 00:21:59.497
and observe the impacts
of our manipulations.

00:21:59.497 --> 00:22:02.356
And this in its turn tells
us something important

00:22:02.356 --> 00:22:05.161
about how we should think of
the metaphysics of causation

00:22:05.161 --> 00:22:07.770
or better I would say.

00:22:07.770 --> 00:22:10.742
the physical substructure
that supports causal belief.

00:22:10.742 --> 00:22:15.742
And it's this in a world that
is as complicated as ours

00:22:16.327 --> 00:22:17.676
and which decomposes

00:22:17.676 --> 00:22:20.995
into a collection of
rule govern components,

00:22:20.995 --> 00:22:23.537
where the laws governing configurations

00:22:23.537 --> 00:22:26.504
can be given in terms of
laws governing components

00:22:26.504 --> 00:22:28.274
and their interactions.

00:22:28.274 --> 00:22:31.190
Causal information can be extracted

00:22:31.190 --> 00:22:35.436
from laws governing components
and the interactions.

00:22:35.436 --> 00:22:39.570
One can concoct very
simple examples, sorry,

00:22:39.570 --> 00:22:40.911
a very simple world.

00:22:40.911 --> 00:22:43.269
A worlds in which there are
a normal logical restrictions

00:22:43.269 --> 00:22:47.230
that is law like restrictions
on initial conditions

00:22:47.230 --> 00:22:50.217
in which causal information
cannot be extracted

00:22:50.217 --> 00:22:52.626
from the global laws.

00:22:52.626 --> 00:22:57.084
But what's really going on
is that in those worlds,

00:22:57.084 --> 00:23:00.870
the global dynamic laws will
not allow us to recover laws,

00:23:00.870 --> 00:23:03.631
governing components and interactions.

00:23:03.631 --> 00:23:07.290
So long as one is willing to
take the local non-biological

00:23:07.290 --> 00:23:10.180
structure of the world, that as basic,

00:23:10.180 --> 00:23:13.310
one can ground facts about
the results of hypothetical

00:23:13.310 --> 00:23:15.380
interventions in that structure,

00:23:15.380 --> 00:23:17.837
that is to say in the laws
that govern the components,

00:23:17.837 --> 00:23:20.860
deriving the global laws from restrictions

00:23:20.860 --> 00:23:22.837
on configurations of components.

00:23:22.837 --> 00:23:25.340
And indeed that that follows

00:23:25.340 --> 00:23:26.800
the standard practice in physics.

00:23:26.800 --> 00:23:29.950
So when you learn Newtonian
physics, for example,

00:23:29.950 --> 00:23:32.312
you learn that an object
will not change its motion

00:23:32.312 --> 00:23:34.146
unless a force acts on it.

00:23:34.146 --> 00:23:36.984
That the force on an object
is equal to it's mass

00:23:36.984 --> 00:23:40.989
times acceleration, and that
went to objects interact,

00:23:40.989 --> 00:23:44.590
they exert forces on one
another equal magnitude

00:23:44.590 --> 00:23:45.540
and opposite direction.

00:23:45.540 --> 00:23:46.740
So what you learn,

00:23:46.740 --> 00:23:48.864
is really the laws
governing the components.

00:23:48.864 --> 00:23:50.745
You piece together an understanding

00:23:50.745 --> 00:23:54.337
of the equations of motion
for the universe as a whole,

00:23:54.337 --> 00:23:57.527
from an understanding
of what its parts are

00:23:57.527 --> 00:24:00.077
and the laws that govern the components.

00:24:00.077 --> 00:24:03.050
So I think it's a sort of
perversion of the content

00:24:03.050 --> 00:24:06.020
of physical theories to make
a lunch for the global laws,

00:24:06.020 --> 00:24:09.667
and then insist that the full
modal content of the theory

00:24:09.667 --> 00:24:13.829
has to be somehow extractable
from the form that they take

00:24:13.829 --> 00:24:16.030
for our world,

00:24:16.030 --> 00:24:18.860
for the particular
configurations of components

00:24:18.860 --> 00:24:20.223
that makes up our world.

00:24:21.960 --> 00:24:24.353
Now, the relations captured in DAGs

00:24:24.353 --> 00:24:27.820
there's intervention
is causal relationships

00:24:27.820 --> 00:24:30.823
are experimentally accessible.

00:24:30.823 --> 00:24:33.858
They're often first in
the order of discovery,

00:24:33.858 --> 00:24:37.350
they're part of the surface
fabric of the world,

00:24:37.350 --> 00:24:39.602
and almost tailor fit to play the role

00:24:39.602 --> 00:24:42.210
that Cartwright
highlighted when she argued

00:24:42.210 --> 00:24:44.462
for the indispensability
of causal relations

00:24:44.462 --> 00:24:46.291
in practical reasoning.

00:24:46.291 --> 00:24:48.960
But there's another tradition

00:24:48.960 --> 00:24:51.100
that comes out of the later Russel

00:24:51.100 --> 00:24:53.950
in this case, by way of the philosophers

00:24:53.950 --> 00:24:58.630
Reichenbach, and Nathan,
sorry, Wesley Salmon.

00:24:58.630 --> 00:25:00.490
And that receives its fullest development

00:25:00.490 --> 00:25:02.040
in Phil Dowe's work.

00:25:02.040 --> 00:25:05.841
And this tradition
analyzes causal relations

00:25:05.841 --> 00:25:08.115
in terms of what he
calls causal processes.

00:25:08.115 --> 00:25:11.482
So causal processes are chains of events

00:25:11.482 --> 00:25:13.958
that are related by interactions

00:25:13.958 --> 00:25:18.082
where a causal interaction
is an interaction between

00:25:18.082 --> 00:25:23.082
events or objects that
involves the local exchange

00:25:24.490 --> 00:25:26.036
of a conserved quantity.

00:25:26.036 --> 00:25:31.036
Causal processes can be defined
more directly in terms of

00:25:31.080 --> 00:25:32.138
micro dynamical loss.

00:25:32.138 --> 00:25:33.694
So cause the process.

00:25:33.694 --> 00:25:37.523
So this is a way of trying
to introduce a causal notion

00:25:37.523 --> 00:25:40.030
that one could connect more directly

00:25:40.030 --> 00:25:41.920
to the sorts of dynamic laws

00:25:41.920 --> 00:25:43.359
that are given by Newtonian physics.

00:25:43.359 --> 00:25:47.223
Causal process and
interventionists accounts

00:25:47.223 --> 00:25:50.945
are sometimes treated as
competing accounts of causation,

00:25:50.945 --> 00:25:53.128
but we don't need to choose between them.

00:25:53.128 --> 00:25:54.930
They're both useful.

00:25:54.930 --> 00:25:58.856
And in classical physics we
find interpretations or examples

00:25:58.856 --> 00:26:00.660
of both interventionists,

00:26:00.660 --> 00:26:03.430
causal relationships and causal processes.

00:26:03.430 --> 00:26:05.964
In the Dowe tradition.

00:26:05.964 --> 00:26:09.540
We have causal processes
at the fundamental level,

00:26:09.540 --> 00:26:12.240
and we have the kind of
manipulable causal relationships

00:26:12.240 --> 00:26:15.364
captured by DAGs all over the place.

00:26:15.364 --> 00:26:19.560
These relationships are
undiscriminating about levels.

00:26:19.560 --> 00:26:21.465
They're neutral about
underlying processes.

00:26:21.465 --> 00:26:25.060
They represent often
local scaffolded relations

00:26:25.060 --> 00:26:26.210
among variables

00:26:26.210 --> 00:26:28.893
that can be investigated experimentally

00:26:28.893 --> 00:26:31.977
frequently without knowledge
of the underlying processes.

00:26:31.977 --> 00:26:36.220
Both moreover, that is both
the relationships captured

00:26:36.220 --> 00:26:38.328
by DAGs and causal processes.

00:26:38.328 --> 00:26:41.260
Both of those notions capture some aspects

00:26:41.260 --> 00:26:44.090
of our everyday notion of cause

00:26:44.090 --> 00:26:45.547
the interventionists relate notion

00:26:45.547 --> 00:26:48.679
captures the idea that you
have a causal connection.

00:26:48.679 --> 00:26:52.698
When one thing can be used
to bring about another.

00:26:52.698 --> 00:26:54.712
Causal processes on the other hand

00:26:54.712 --> 00:26:58.010
capture the idea that influences

00:26:58.010 --> 00:27:00.890
propagate through space

00:27:00.890 --> 00:27:03.510
causal influences propagate through space

00:27:03.510 --> 00:27:05.450
and that they satisfy the expectation

00:27:05.450 --> 00:27:08.928
that causal connections should
be mediated by mechanisms

00:27:08.928 --> 00:27:11.780
and that they involve
some sort of contact,

00:27:11.780 --> 00:27:15.333
some sort of local bumping
up against one another.

00:27:15.333 --> 00:27:19.890
So if intervention is causal relationships

00:27:19.890 --> 00:27:21.880
are part of the surface
fabric of the world.

00:27:21.880 --> 00:27:23.748
Then you can think of causal processes

00:27:23.748 --> 00:27:27.641
as part of the deep theoretically
postulated substructure

00:27:27.641 --> 00:27:32.223
that supports the high level
relationships captured by DAGs.

00:27:34.850 --> 00:27:37.690
So I said that causal physics

00:27:37.690 --> 00:27:39.843
gives us both causal processes

00:27:39.843 --> 00:27:43.023
and the high level causal
networks captured by DAGs.

00:27:43.023 --> 00:27:45.330
But there's a puzzle
about the relationship

00:27:45.330 --> 00:27:47.662
between the sort of high
level causal pathways

00:27:47.662 --> 00:27:50.784
and the underlying dynamical processes,

00:27:50.784 --> 00:27:53.442
that's crucial to
understanding causal notions

00:27:53.442 --> 00:27:55.309
of both kinds.

00:27:55.309 --> 00:27:58.100
So interventionists causal relationships

00:27:58.100 --> 00:28:01.794
of the kind captured in DAGs
have one striking feature

00:28:01.794 --> 00:28:04.763
they're temporarily asymmetric.

00:28:04.763 --> 00:28:09.066
So they run in one direction,
from past to future.

00:28:09.066 --> 00:28:11.180
And that temporarily symmetry

00:28:11.180 --> 00:28:13.740
is no mere matter of convention.

00:28:13.740 --> 00:28:15.930
If we follow Cartwright in saying

00:28:15.930 --> 00:28:17.861
that was definitive of
causal relationships,

00:28:17.861 --> 00:28:21.855
as distinct from mere
probabilistic correlations,

00:28:21.855 --> 00:28:24.760
is that they can function
as strategic groups

00:28:24.760 --> 00:28:26.079
to bringing about ends.

00:28:26.079 --> 00:28:29.340
Then it's manifest and
pervasive empirical fact

00:28:29.340 --> 00:28:30.500
about our world

00:28:30.500 --> 00:28:33.124
that all known strategic
routes to bring about ends

00:28:33.124 --> 00:28:35.874
run from past to future.

00:28:35.874 --> 00:28:38.500
- [Priya] So Jenann if I may interrupt

00:28:38.500 --> 00:28:39.901
to have a quick question.

00:28:39.901 --> 00:28:44.320
So in the absence of micro physical laws,

00:28:44.320 --> 00:28:47.650
does it mean that causation
is not well-defined

00:28:47.650 --> 00:28:49.415
or it cannot be established?

00:28:49.415 --> 00:28:51.427
So I'm thinking of something like gravity.

00:28:51.427 --> 00:28:55.863
So you do not have micro physical laws.

00:28:59.001 --> 00:29:02.821
So I'm curious is that
in the previous slide

00:29:02.821 --> 00:29:05.970
you had that as one of the points.

00:29:05.970 --> 00:29:09.333
- So that's true of causal processes.

00:29:09.333 --> 00:29:10.897
- [Priya] I see.

00:29:10.897 --> 00:29:12.487
- So you're right quantum mechanics,

00:29:12.487 --> 00:29:16.905
there's no mechanism for the
propagation of gravitation

00:29:16.905 --> 00:29:18.470
and that's right.

00:29:18.470 --> 00:29:20.242
So according to causal process account,

00:29:20.242 --> 00:29:25.200
there's no causal link between sort of

00:29:25.200 --> 00:29:27.104
there's no causal
mechanism or causal process

00:29:27.104 --> 00:29:30.297
by which gravitational
influence is propagating.

00:29:30.297 --> 00:29:33.022
- [Priya] Thank you for that.

00:29:33.022 --> 00:29:34.008
- Sure.

00:29:34.008 --> 00:29:36.703
It's a good question. Thank you for that.

00:29:39.910 --> 00:29:42.643
So, let me go back to, okay.

00:29:46.000 --> 00:29:48.517
So as I'm saying that
the temporally symmetric,

00:29:48.517 --> 00:29:51.000
is no matter of convention

00:29:51.000 --> 00:29:53.500
and indeed of all of the
reasons that Russel gave

00:29:53.500 --> 00:29:55.740
in that early paper for
saying that causation

00:29:55.740 --> 00:29:59.100
had been purged from physics
at the fundamental level,

00:29:59.100 --> 00:30:01.045
this is the one that
persuaded most people.

00:30:01.045 --> 00:30:05.340
The underlying dynamic laws
are simply equations of motion

00:30:05.340 --> 00:30:07.343
that relate the state of
the universe at one time

00:30:07.343 --> 00:30:08.842
to say that the others,

00:30:08.842 --> 00:30:12.970
they have no intrinsic
direction of determination.

00:30:12.970 --> 00:30:16.172
So they couldn't in that sense
be properly called causes.

00:30:16.172 --> 00:30:20.397
So now this gives rise
to a straight forward

00:30:20.397 --> 00:30:21.947
physical puzzle however,

00:30:21.947 --> 00:30:25.364
how do the kinds of high
level causal pathways

00:30:25.364 --> 00:30:28.581
that have set an objective
part of the surface fabric

00:30:28.581 --> 00:30:32.507
of our world arise from
temporally symmetric

00:30:32.507 --> 00:30:34.353
underlying laws.

00:30:34.353 --> 00:30:36.958
Now this isn't the kind of question

00:30:36.958 --> 00:30:40.920
that people in the lab studying
signal pathways and cells,

00:30:40.920 --> 00:30:42.374
and so on typically ask.

00:30:42.374 --> 00:30:45.722
Scientists who empirically
investigate the causal structure,

00:30:45.722 --> 00:30:49.380
who, sorry, who impaired the
investigate causal structure

00:30:49.380 --> 00:30:51.053
of particular kinds of systems

00:30:51.053 --> 00:30:53.098
take it for granted that
causal relations run

00:30:53.098 --> 00:30:55.130
from past to future.

00:30:55.130 --> 00:30:56.760
So they set up experiments

00:30:56.760 --> 00:30:58.600
that allow localized interventions

00:30:58.600 --> 00:31:01.908
and they look for the
temporarily downstream effects.

00:31:01.908 --> 00:31:06.080
But it is a theoretical
question for the physicist

00:31:06.080 --> 00:31:08.010
trying to relate the
kinds of causal pathways

00:31:08.010 --> 00:31:12.926
cited in for example, biology
labs to an underlying theory.

00:31:12.926 --> 00:31:14.482
And as it happens,

00:31:14.482 --> 00:31:17.970
it's a question that's been
illuminated by research

00:31:17.970 --> 00:31:20.800
into the foundations of thermodynamics.

00:31:20.800 --> 00:31:23.788
So for those of you who
don't know the history

00:31:23.788 --> 00:31:26.730
that research began in mid 19th century,

00:31:26.730 --> 00:31:29.314
and it was originally
focused on trying to derive

00:31:29.314 --> 00:31:31.500
the phenomenological asymmetries

00:31:31.500 --> 00:31:33.651
embodied in the second
law of thermodynamics

00:31:33.651 --> 00:31:37.950
from time symmetric laws
of classical mechanics.

00:31:37.950 --> 00:31:40.140
And it turned only quite recently

00:31:40.140 --> 00:31:41.737
into a very general account

00:31:41.737 --> 00:31:45.620
of the sources of temporal
asymmetry in our world.

00:31:45.620 --> 00:31:48.850
So a conversation that
started by being about why,

00:31:48.850 --> 00:31:52.370
for example, a gas will disperse
to fill an open container,

00:31:52.370 --> 00:31:55.975
but not collect
spontaneously in one corner

00:31:55.975 --> 00:32:00.050
or why ice melts when placed
in a warm glass of water.

00:32:00.050 --> 00:32:03.250
But we don't see ice
cube spontaneously form

00:32:03.250 --> 00:32:06.207
out of warm water without
a change in temperature.

00:32:06.207 --> 00:32:09.974
How that turned into a conversation
about why, for example,

00:32:09.974 --> 00:32:12.263
we remember the past, but not the future

00:32:12.263 --> 00:32:14.873
and why time seems to
flow from past to future,

00:32:14.873 --> 00:32:18.272
and why causation doesn't run backwards.

00:32:18.272 --> 00:32:22.794
And as in the revolution in
our thinking about causation

00:32:22.794 --> 00:32:25.270
that I said, came with the development

00:32:25.270 --> 00:32:27.641
of the structural causal
modeling framework.

00:32:27.641 --> 00:32:30.237
There was input from both
scientists and philosophers

00:32:30.237 --> 00:32:33.800
that was sort of instrumental
to the development.

00:32:33.800 --> 00:32:35.070
So for example,

00:32:35.070 --> 00:32:37.980
there was Boltzmann and Maxwell
and Gibbs, all scientists,

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:41.490
but there was also Reichenbach,
David Albert, Huw Price,

00:32:41.490 --> 00:32:44.710
all of course philosophers.

00:32:44.710 --> 00:32:47.640
So there remains a lot of
dispute in the foundations

00:32:47.640 --> 00:32:50.034
of thermodynamics in
dispute in particular,

00:32:50.034 --> 00:32:53.490
about how to understand
some of the most important

00:32:53.490 --> 00:32:57.510
philosophically important
temporal asymmetries.

00:32:57.510 --> 00:33:00.610
But it was really David Albert's account

00:33:00.610 --> 00:33:04.640
from this book in 2000 that
brought the foundations

00:33:04.640 --> 00:33:07.174
of thermodynamics into the
kind of conceptual focus

00:33:07.174 --> 00:33:11.500
that made it possible to
really attack these questions.

00:33:11.500 --> 00:33:12.868
So Albert's account

00:33:12.868 --> 00:33:17.322
that is philosophically
interesting temporal asymmetries.

00:33:17.322 --> 00:33:20.240
So Albert's account has
three first principles,

00:33:20.240 --> 00:33:23.130
there is the Newtonian laws of motion.

00:33:23.130 --> 00:33:25.261
There's a Statistical Postulate,

00:33:25.261 --> 00:33:28.778
and there's something that
he calls the Past-Hypothesis.

00:33:28.778 --> 00:33:30.390
So here's how it works.

00:33:30.390 --> 00:33:31.646
Newtonian laws of motion,

00:33:31.646 --> 00:33:33.955
are just the familiar time symmetric laws

00:33:33.955 --> 00:33:36.268
that Russell was referring to.

00:33:36.268 --> 00:33:39.716
This Statistical Postulate
is a probability distribution

00:33:39.716 --> 00:33:42.569
that assigns probability distribution

00:33:42.569 --> 00:33:45.128
to assist them being
in a given microstate,

00:33:45.128 --> 00:33:47.570
given its macrostate.

00:33:47.570 --> 00:33:50.390
Macrostate is just given by the values

00:33:50.390 --> 00:33:51.670
of thermodynamic parameters.

00:33:51.670 --> 00:33:56.670
The Statistical Postulate is
strict addition to the law,

00:33:57.310 --> 00:33:59.452
so it doesn't follow from them directly,

00:33:59.452 --> 00:34:04.310
but there are a lot of attempts
to motivate it dynamically.

00:34:04.310 --> 00:34:06.987
The Past-Hypothesis, the
third of these postulate,

00:34:06.987 --> 00:34:09.570
is a contingent hypothesis

00:34:09.570 --> 00:34:11.544
about the early history of the world.

00:34:11.544 --> 00:34:13.411
It says that the universe started

00:34:13.411 --> 00:34:15.720
in a state of low enough entropy,

00:34:15.720 --> 00:34:18.076
to make thermodynamic generalizations

00:34:18.076 --> 00:34:20.950
applicable for the
roughly 15 billion years,

00:34:20.950 --> 00:34:25.180
that we think that those
generalizations have held.

00:34:25.180 --> 00:34:27.550
And here's how the account goes.

00:34:27.550 --> 00:34:29.852
The three postulates
work together as follows.

00:34:29.852 --> 00:34:31.766
Newtonian dynamical laws

00:34:31.766 --> 00:34:35.417
do limit the space of
physically possible worlds.

00:34:35.417 --> 00:34:39.167
The Past-Hypothesis knocks
out all of those worlds

00:34:39.167 --> 00:34:42.628
that don't start out
in a low entropy state.

00:34:42.628 --> 00:34:45.720
And then if you take
the initial microstates

00:34:45.720 --> 00:34:47.267
of the remaining worlds,

00:34:47.267 --> 00:34:50.996
and you apply the
Newtonian dynamical laws,

00:34:50.996 --> 00:34:53.830
you will see that some
of those remaining worlds

00:34:53.830 --> 00:34:57.193
will be on what are called
entropy increasing projectories.

00:34:57.193 --> 00:35:00.660
That is to say that their
entropy will be increasing

00:35:00.660 --> 00:35:03.200
from that initial state over its history,

00:35:03.200 --> 00:35:05.020
until it reaches equilibrium.

00:35:05.020 --> 00:35:08.950
And some of them will be on
entropy decreasing trajectories.

00:35:08.950 --> 00:35:11.190
That is where the entropy will decrease

00:35:11.190 --> 00:35:12.421
from the initial state.

00:35:12.421 --> 00:35:14.255
As far as the dynamical laws go,

00:35:14.255 --> 00:35:18.440
there will be worlds of both
kinds in the remaining set,

00:35:18.440 --> 00:35:21.030
the statistical postulate,
then we'll enter in

00:35:21.030 --> 00:35:24.650
and provide a probability
distribution over the worlds,

00:35:24.650 --> 00:35:26.476
that overwhelmingly favors those

00:35:26.476 --> 00:35:29.299
on entropy increasing trajectory.

00:35:29.299 --> 00:35:31.745
So the result of these three things

00:35:31.745 --> 00:35:35.340
is that the most probable and
not just the most probable,

00:35:35.340 --> 00:35:39.216
but the overwhelmingly most
probable history of the universe

00:35:39.216 --> 00:35:41.873
is one where in entropy rises.

00:35:43.140 --> 00:35:47.055
Now, Albert himself gives the
account at the global level

00:35:47.055 --> 00:35:48.220
where I mean,

00:35:48.220 --> 00:35:51.900
he presents it in exactly
the way that I just said,

00:35:51.900 --> 00:35:54.112
that you have a probability
distribution over worlds

00:35:54.112 --> 00:35:59.112
and you apply the whole
account at the level of worlds

00:35:59.380 --> 00:36:00.580
as a whole.

00:36:00.580 --> 00:36:01.656
One need not do that.

00:36:01.656 --> 00:36:06.580
Formally it works just as well
for some local adiabatically

00:36:06.580 --> 00:36:08.500
isolated subsystem of the world.

00:36:08.500 --> 00:36:11.153
That is a system that's
not exchanging energy

00:36:11.153 --> 00:36:13.028
with its environment.

00:36:13.028 --> 00:36:15.852
And indeed, there are
good reasons for thinking

00:36:15.852 --> 00:36:19.452
that the story might best
be told at that level.

00:36:19.452 --> 00:36:20.560
Why?

00:36:20.560 --> 00:36:22.819
Because those are the systems
to which thermodynamics

00:36:22.819 --> 00:36:24.645
is typically applied.

00:36:24.645 --> 00:36:29.215
And second is not obvious
whether the local story follows

00:36:29.215 --> 00:36:30.734
from the global one.

00:36:30.734 --> 00:36:33.540
I think it does, but it's not,

00:36:33.540 --> 00:36:36.376
it's not as perspicuous as if
you give the story directly

00:36:36.376 --> 00:36:37.975
at the local level.

00:36:37.975 --> 00:36:41.350
And finally, the probabilities
have a much clearer

00:36:41.350 --> 00:36:44.062
interpretation if the story
is told at the local level.

00:36:44.062 --> 00:36:47.474
So if you look at Albert's postulates,

00:36:47.474 --> 00:36:51.450
the Newtonian laws are times symmetric.

00:36:51.450 --> 00:36:56.450
There is no direction of
determination built in to them.

00:36:56.870 --> 00:36:59.649
And the Statistical Postulate,
is also time independent.

00:36:59.649 --> 00:37:02.637
So the only time asymmetric assumption

00:37:02.637 --> 00:37:04.453
is the Past-Hypothesis.

00:37:04.453 --> 00:37:07.620
You know that the initial
state of the world

00:37:07.620 --> 00:37:08.453
was low entropy,

00:37:08.453 --> 00:37:11.900
but there's no corresponding
boundary condition

00:37:11.900 --> 00:37:12.935
in the future.

00:37:12.935 --> 00:37:15.923
And it's a particularly
crucial part of the story

00:37:15.923 --> 00:37:18.750
because it's doing all
of the explanatory work

00:37:18.750 --> 00:37:23.000
in terms of deriving the
temporarily symmetries.

00:37:26.755 --> 00:37:30.210
There's been some important
discussion about the right form

00:37:30.210 --> 00:37:31.209
for that hypothesis.

00:37:31.209 --> 00:37:34.475
Everybody agrees that some
kind of temporal asymmetry

00:37:34.475 --> 00:37:37.115
in boundary conditions needs to be assumed

00:37:37.115 --> 00:37:41.430
to extract a temporal
asymmetries of thermodynamics.

00:37:41.430 --> 00:37:43.570
In Albert's account is low entropy state

00:37:43.570 --> 00:37:46.156
for the universe that
sometime it's this and past.

00:37:46.156 --> 00:37:50.370
Mathias Frisch, in a discussion
of the asymmetries embodied

00:37:50.370 --> 00:37:51.649
in causal reasoning,

00:37:51.649 --> 00:37:54.170
has shown that there are
different ways of expressing

00:37:54.170 --> 00:37:55.624
the time asymmetric assumptions,

00:37:55.624 --> 00:37:58.297
that are needed to generate
the sorts of asymmetries

00:37:58.297 --> 00:38:00.573
that would support causal inferences.

00:38:00.573 --> 00:38:03.780
So he points out that it would
do just as well, for example,

00:38:03.780 --> 00:38:06.087
to assume a Causal Markov Condition,

00:38:06.087 --> 00:38:09.860
that is that any node in a dag
is conditionally independent

00:38:09.860 --> 00:38:13.511
of its non-descendants given its parents,

00:38:13.511 --> 00:38:15.987
or as an assumption about
microscopic randomness

00:38:15.987 --> 00:38:20.743
in the initial, but not
final state of the system.

00:38:20.743 --> 00:38:23.550
I think there's no substantive question

00:38:23.550 --> 00:38:26.590
about which of these
assumptions is more fundamental.

00:38:26.590 --> 00:38:28.756
You can derive them from one another.

00:38:28.756 --> 00:38:31.466
There are different ways of
describing the structures,

00:38:31.466 --> 00:38:35.410
that is to say the contingent
asymmetries in our world

00:38:35.410 --> 00:38:38.941
that support causal inferences
running from past to future.

00:38:38.941 --> 00:38:42.418
Others have pointed out that
in the explanatory story

00:38:42.418 --> 00:38:44.660
what's really needed to generate

00:38:44.660 --> 00:38:46.226
thermodynamic generalizations

00:38:46.226 --> 00:38:49.290
is not really an initial low entropy,

00:38:49.290 --> 00:38:52.950
but the randomness or
uniformity the early universe

00:38:52.950 --> 00:38:54.500
that matters.

00:38:54.500 --> 00:38:58.480
So while there's a lot sort
of to sort it out here,

00:38:58.480 --> 00:39:00.500
what there is broad agreement

00:39:00.500 --> 00:39:03.055
that pending the mathematical conjectures

00:39:03.055 --> 00:39:05.350
on which this account rests,

00:39:05.350 --> 00:39:07.992
the local macroscopic asymmetries

00:39:07.992 --> 00:39:10.021
in our part of the universe,

00:39:10.021 --> 00:39:13.188
have their origin in a fact
about the state of our universe

00:39:13.188 --> 00:39:14.525
in the distant past,

00:39:14.525 --> 00:39:17.842
that developed under
effective the classical laws

00:39:17.842 --> 00:39:20.670
into a world with the sorts of emergent

00:39:20.670 --> 00:39:21.898
macroscopic asymmetries

00:39:21.898 --> 00:39:26.459
captured in thermodynamic generalizations.

00:39:26.459 --> 00:39:27.924
Now, if one is interested

00:39:27.924 --> 00:39:31.603
in how this story connects
specifically to causal inference,

00:39:31.603 --> 00:39:33.890
one will be interested in

00:39:35.760 --> 00:39:38.720
some different ways of
stating the assumptions

00:39:38.720 --> 00:39:40.859
that are needed to derive the asymmetries.

00:39:40.859 --> 00:39:44.024
If one's more interested
in Cartwright's idea,

00:39:44.024 --> 00:39:46.371
that if you're given a
body of correlations,

00:39:46.371 --> 00:39:49.968
the causal pathways are
the ones that can be used

00:39:49.968 --> 00:39:52.410
as strategic groups to bring about ends.

00:39:52.410 --> 00:39:55.435
Here's how to get that
in a way that connects

00:39:55.435 --> 00:39:57.912
more naturally to that idea.

00:39:57.912 --> 00:40:00.517
So this is a conception of causation

00:40:00.517 --> 00:40:04.910
that brings the connection
to agency into the foreground

00:40:04.910 --> 00:40:07.719
and connects to people's
pre theoretic idea,

00:40:07.719 --> 00:40:09.940
that causation is
something that you can use

00:40:09.940 --> 00:40:12.003
to bring about ends.

00:40:13.603 --> 00:40:18.206
If we start in a world with
the thermodynamic gradient

00:40:18.206 --> 00:40:22.270
and we hold fixed all of
the information embodied

00:40:22.270 --> 00:40:25.860
in the present surveyable
macroscopic state of the world.

00:40:25.860 --> 00:40:28.720
So the kind of information
that an agent has

00:40:28.720 --> 00:40:30.400
just by looking around.

00:40:30.400 --> 00:40:32.930
And here, I don't mean just what you get

00:40:32.930 --> 00:40:34.068
by looking around you

00:40:34.068 --> 00:40:36.560
and holding fix the ambient temperature

00:40:36.560 --> 00:40:38.907
and dispositions of
medium-sized dry goods.

00:40:38.907 --> 00:40:42.160
I mean, you hold fixed all
of the information embodied

00:40:42.160 --> 00:40:44.568
in libraries, scientific databases,

00:40:44.568 --> 00:40:47.206
all of the information
that we've somehow amassed

00:40:47.206 --> 00:40:51.610
and recorded, or that we could
have accessed and recorded.

00:40:51.610 --> 00:40:54.730
And we ask what's the
probabilistic effect,

00:40:54.730 --> 00:40:57.720
holding all of that fix,
of local interventions

00:40:57.720 --> 00:41:00.180
on the present state of the environment

00:41:00.180 --> 00:41:02.635
of a kind that are
possible in practical terms

00:41:02.635 --> 00:41:04.356
for agents like us?

00:41:04.356 --> 00:41:08.657
So you manipulate some feature
of your local environment

00:41:08.657 --> 00:41:11.449
and you apply the probability postulate

00:41:11.449 --> 00:41:14.110
to obtain the probability distribution

00:41:14.110 --> 00:41:17.172
over the possible microstate of the world.

00:41:17.172 --> 00:41:20.582
And then you evolve that
forward or backward in time

00:41:20.582 --> 00:41:23.599
using the classical mechanical laws.

00:41:23.599 --> 00:41:25.170
Here's what happened,

00:41:25.170 --> 00:41:27.170
if you just evolving
it forward or backward

00:41:27.170 --> 00:41:29.169
with the classical micro mechanical laws,

00:41:29.169 --> 00:41:30.652
you can't really predict much

00:41:30.652 --> 00:41:33.043
about either the past or the future.

00:41:33.043 --> 00:41:33.876
Why?

00:41:33.876 --> 00:41:36.180
Because there are lots of different ways

00:41:36.180 --> 00:41:37.130
that the world could be.

00:41:37.130 --> 00:41:39.354
Everything depends on
the actual microstate

00:41:39.354 --> 00:41:42.558
of the universe, and you
don't need to just know

00:41:42.558 --> 00:41:44.565
what's going on locally
microscopically here,

00:41:44.565 --> 00:41:48.678
you need to know what's
going on everywhere.

00:41:48.678 --> 00:41:51.820
You need the micro state
of the universe as a whole

00:41:51.820 --> 00:41:53.602
to predict anything
with the classical laws.

00:41:53.602 --> 00:41:58.602
If however we conditionalize
on low entropy state

00:42:00.800 --> 00:42:01.892
in the distant past,

00:42:01.892 --> 00:42:04.019
then the probabilities of past events

00:42:04.019 --> 00:42:07.864
of which we have any kind
of macroscopic record

00:42:07.864 --> 00:42:09.583
will be unaltered,

00:42:09.583 --> 00:42:13.090
but the intervention will
raise the probability

00:42:13.090 --> 00:42:15.540
of certain kinds of
macroscopic featured in this.

00:42:16.838 --> 00:42:19.603
Now the backward part of this,

00:42:19.603 --> 00:42:22.830
that is the question of what
makes something a record.

00:42:22.830 --> 00:42:25.499
And the reason that condition
lies under low entropy past

00:42:25.499 --> 00:42:29.340
makes the macroscopic
present full of records.

00:42:29.340 --> 00:42:33.081
That's something that's
been extensively studied.

00:42:33.081 --> 00:42:36.230
So if you went to Carlo
Rovelli's talk, for example,

00:42:36.230 --> 00:42:39.430
the first in the series that I mentioned,

00:42:39.430 --> 00:42:41.205
he did a beautiful job of making explicit

00:42:41.205 --> 00:42:43.360
the physics of that.

00:42:43.360 --> 00:42:44.700
But the way to think of it

00:42:50.060 --> 00:42:52.560
and the way that one has to think of,

00:42:52.560 --> 00:42:54.270
there's a little bit more
that needs to be added here,

00:42:54.270 --> 00:42:55.690
which is that one has to
think of the internal,

00:42:55.690 --> 00:42:58.520
one has to think in a
certain way of the internal

00:42:58.520 --> 00:43:00.028
dynamics of the agent.

00:43:00.028 --> 00:43:03.057
What I'm trying to show you
is how all of these pieces

00:43:03.057 --> 00:43:06.480
fit together conceptually
at the macro scale,

00:43:06.480 --> 00:43:08.014
to help us understand causation.

00:43:08.014 --> 00:43:09.633
And the way to think of it,

00:43:09.633 --> 00:43:14.150
to get the causal stuff into it,

00:43:14.150 --> 00:43:16.697
is that the causal asymmetry
is really the flip side

00:43:16.697 --> 00:43:18.823
of the asymmetry of records.

00:43:18.823 --> 00:43:21.663
So when you intervene in
the local environment,

00:43:21.663 --> 00:43:26.033
there will be traces in the
future of your intervention.

00:43:29.070 --> 00:43:31.150
What you'll be doing is
you'll be doing some work.

00:43:31.150 --> 00:43:32.430
You jump into a swimming pool,

00:43:32.430 --> 00:43:34.260
you leave a footprint in the sand,

00:43:34.260 --> 00:43:35.970
you dig a ditch or you build a house.

00:43:35.970 --> 00:43:38.300
And what you're doing from
a physical perspective

00:43:38.300 --> 00:43:41.432
is you're creating an ordered
state of the environment

00:43:41.432 --> 00:43:44.454
that will take some time to decay.

00:43:44.454 --> 00:43:47.323
So what you think of as
the effects of your actions

00:43:47.323 --> 00:43:50.488
are the future record of its occurrence.

00:43:50.488 --> 00:43:53.730
That means if we look
for the strategic routes

00:43:53.730 --> 00:43:55.110
for bringing about ends.

00:43:55.110 --> 00:43:57.790
If we look for ways of
doing something in the here

00:43:57.790 --> 00:44:00.823
and now that will make a
difference to probabilities

00:44:00.823 --> 00:44:02.174
of future events,

00:44:02.174 --> 00:44:05.028
of probabilities of events
at other times and places,

00:44:05.028 --> 00:44:08.480
we will find that there's
nothing that one can do

00:44:08.480 --> 00:44:09.942
to make a difference to the past,

00:44:09.942 --> 00:44:13.070
but there's a lot that we
can do to make a difference

00:44:13.070 --> 00:44:14.467
to the future.

00:44:14.467 --> 00:44:17.480
So the causal asymmetry,

00:44:17.480 --> 00:44:21.675
is just the flip side of
the asymmetry of records.

00:44:21.675 --> 00:44:25.180
What you're doing when you
act in the here and now

00:44:25.180 --> 00:44:30.180
is you're creating future
records of your present action.

00:44:30.189 --> 00:44:34.380
Now so far, everything that I've said

00:44:34.380 --> 00:44:37.049
stays at the level of physical structure.

00:44:37.049 --> 00:44:39.676
We've talked about the
temporarily symmetric laws,

00:44:39.676 --> 00:44:43.220
about the asymmetric intervention pathways

00:44:43.220 --> 00:44:45.754
that can be studied in the
lab and captured in dyads.

00:44:45.754 --> 00:44:48.530
We have the external
infrastructure in place

00:44:48.530 --> 00:44:51.223
that just grabs the macroscopic
fabric of the world.

00:44:52.376 --> 00:44:55.893
Now we're in a position to
talk about causal concepts.

00:44:55.893 --> 00:44:59.180
So we've moved from talking
about structure in the world

00:44:59.180 --> 00:45:04.100
that is external infrastructure
in the environment,

00:45:04.100 --> 00:45:07.610
to talking about something
that has a role in an agent's

00:45:07.610 --> 00:45:09.424
internal cognitive economy.

00:45:09.424 --> 00:45:14.180
To do that, we need to introduce an agent.

00:45:14.180 --> 00:45:17.320
And we couple the agent to
the macroscopic structure

00:45:17.320 --> 00:45:18.380
of the world.

00:45:18.380 --> 00:45:19.610
What is an agent?

00:45:19.610 --> 00:45:21.650
An agent is the system
whose job it is to extract

00:45:21.650 --> 00:45:24.091
information from the environment

00:45:24.091 --> 00:45:25.973
and use it to guide behavior.

00:45:27.940 --> 00:45:31.314
Is coupled to the world via
sensory and motor pathways

00:45:31.314 --> 00:45:33.721
that give it macroscopic information

00:45:33.721 --> 00:45:38.700
and sensory feedback on
its own interventions.

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:42.830
And so now we ask how do
causal concepts arise?

00:45:42.830 --> 00:45:44.651
Which is to say, we look at the role

00:45:44.651 --> 00:45:49.397
that those concepts play in an
internal influential network.

00:45:49.397 --> 00:45:51.844
That's linked to perception at one end

00:45:51.844 --> 00:45:53.533
and to action at another.

00:45:54.430 --> 00:45:57.960
So an agent that is an agent like this,

00:45:57.960 --> 00:46:00.790
that's coupled to the environment
by sensors and actuators,

00:46:00.790 --> 00:46:03.113
and it's giving macroscopic information,

00:46:06.110 --> 00:46:10.486
will be interested in
knowing how, what it does

00:46:10.486 --> 00:46:14.680
affects what it sees in causal concepts

00:46:14.680 --> 00:46:18.170
will be defined both by their
connections to perception

00:46:18.170 --> 00:46:19.013
and to action.

00:46:20.050 --> 00:46:22.100
And the sciences that
are relevant to studying

00:46:22.100 --> 00:46:25.040
causal concepts, now
looking not at the world,

00:46:25.040 --> 00:46:27.690
but looking at the agent

00:46:27.690 --> 00:46:30.600
and the way that her
causal concepts are nested

00:46:30.600 --> 00:46:32.530
in her internal economy,

00:46:32.530 --> 00:46:34.752
the sciences that are
relevant to study that,

00:46:34.752 --> 00:46:37.260
are psychology cognitive science.

00:46:37.260 --> 00:46:39.990
And there's just a wealth
of fascinating work

00:46:39.990 --> 00:46:42.400
on causal cognition and learning

00:46:42.400 --> 00:46:44.300
on the development of causal ideas

00:46:44.300 --> 00:46:46.590
on their role in inference, deficient,

00:46:46.590 --> 00:46:48.355
and action in world judgment.

00:46:48.355 --> 00:46:53.355
So if we step back and we
take a kind of cross section

00:46:54.020 --> 00:46:55.991
of the world ordered by scale,

00:46:55.991 --> 00:46:58.503
and we look at the layers of structure

00:46:58.503 --> 00:47:03.080
from the microscopic all the
way up to the macroscopic,

00:47:03.080 --> 00:47:04.840
up to the level of the human being,

00:47:04.840 --> 00:47:07.577
interacting with the
macroscopic environment,

00:47:07.577 --> 00:47:10.333
the picture that sort of coming into focus

00:47:10.333 --> 00:47:12.189
looks something like this,

00:47:12.189 --> 00:47:15.840
the bottom layer, there's
the geometry of space time,

00:47:15.840 --> 00:47:19.570
which in a classical
setting imposes or embodies

00:47:19.570 --> 00:47:22.440
constraints on the causal
connectability view events.

00:47:22.440 --> 00:47:23.273
And by that,

00:47:23.273 --> 00:47:26.583
I mean their connectability
by causal processes.

00:47:26.583 --> 00:47:28.233
There's the matter content.

00:47:28.233 --> 00:47:33.050
And there's the temporarily
symmetric microscopic laws

00:47:33.050 --> 00:47:36.195
that tell us how the matter
in one volume of space-time

00:47:36.195 --> 00:47:38.741
affects matter in other
volume of space-time.

00:47:38.741 --> 00:47:40.950
Or how the state of the world at one time

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:42.327
relates to a state at another.

00:47:42.327 --> 00:47:47.327
And then there's the emergent
macroscopic asymmetries.

00:47:47.512 --> 00:47:49.940
And the macroscopic asymmetries arise.

00:47:49.940 --> 00:47:52.840
You remember because of the
low entropy boundary condition

00:47:52.840 --> 00:47:54.504
at one end of the universe.

00:47:54.504 --> 00:47:56.962
The macroscopic asymmetries in their turn,

00:47:56.962 --> 00:48:01.014
support the emergence of
features that use information

00:48:01.014 --> 00:48:02.883
to guide behavior

00:48:02.883 --> 00:48:06.860
because of the way that
the macroscopic asymmetries

00:48:07.737 --> 00:48:11.380
allow the present state of
the world to contain records.

00:48:11.380 --> 00:48:13.990
Information makes the macroscopic states

00:48:13.990 --> 00:48:16.943
rich with information that agents can use.

00:48:18.780 --> 00:48:21.650
Eventually those agents get complex enough

00:48:21.650 --> 00:48:24.670
that they do something that we
would recognize as thinking,

00:48:24.670 --> 00:48:26.737
and by the time we get
to creatures like us

00:48:26.737 --> 00:48:30.713
thinking is causal from stem to stern.

00:48:31.880 --> 00:48:33.960
Now, while there's not much that's known

00:48:33.960 --> 00:48:37.640
and various points of dispute,
the sketch that I just gave,

00:48:37.640 --> 00:48:41.726
it is robust with respect to
most of the emerging details.

00:48:41.726 --> 00:48:44.130
And notice how much it transforms

00:48:44.130 --> 00:48:46.560
both our understanding of causation

00:48:46.560 --> 00:48:49.450
and our understanding of ourselves

00:48:49.450 --> 00:48:50.902
is very different from anything

00:48:50.902 --> 00:48:53.810
that might've come out
of simply reflection

00:48:53.810 --> 00:48:55.480
on the concept of cause.

00:48:55.480 --> 00:48:57.730
No single part of this story

00:48:57.730 --> 00:48:59.855
is the story of what causes are.

00:48:59.855 --> 00:49:03.270
But I think that once all
of the pieces are assembled,

00:49:03.270 --> 00:49:04.630
there's no further question,

00:49:04.630 --> 00:49:07.470
no story about the
metaphysics of causation

00:49:07.470 --> 00:49:08.853
that needs to be answered.

00:49:10.700 --> 00:49:11.720
There are two questions

00:49:11.720 --> 00:49:14.630
that philosophers always
want to ask about causes.

00:49:14.630 --> 00:49:19.630
They wanna ask are causes
inside or outside the head?

00:49:19.880 --> 00:49:23.030
Are they part of the mind
independent fabric of reality,

00:49:23.030 --> 00:49:25.430
or are they in the eye of the observer?

00:49:25.430 --> 00:49:26.340
And they want to ask,

00:49:26.340 --> 00:49:28.243
where does the asymmetry come from?

00:49:29.530 --> 00:49:31.155
To take the second one first,

00:49:31.155 --> 00:49:33.990
we've just seen a rather complex answer

00:49:33.990 --> 00:49:35.854
to the source of the asymmetry.

00:49:35.854 --> 00:49:39.180
What we saw was that there's
not a single asymmetry,

00:49:39.180 --> 00:49:41.983
but rather a couple of
related asymmetries.

00:49:41.983 --> 00:49:44.060
There's the thermodynamic gradient

00:49:44.060 --> 00:49:46.570
created by low entropy
state of the universe,

00:49:46.570 --> 00:49:48.538
which creates a macroscopic asymmetry

00:49:48.538 --> 00:49:51.560
that in its turn supports the emergence

00:49:51.560 --> 00:49:54.970
of information gathering
and utilizing systems.

00:49:54.970 --> 00:49:57.163
When such systems get sufficiently complex

00:49:57.163 --> 00:49:59.860
information and gathering and utilization

00:49:59.860 --> 00:50:02.962
takes the form of conceptual activity

00:50:02.962 --> 00:50:06.400
creatures like this
develop causal concepts

00:50:06.400 --> 00:50:07.983
with a built-in asymmetry

00:50:07.983 --> 00:50:09.442
that reflects in asymmetry

00:50:09.442 --> 00:50:14.360
in their practical and epistemic
relationship to the world.

00:50:14.360 --> 00:50:17.370
So while there is no
fundamental asymmetric relation

00:50:17.370 --> 00:50:20.531
of determination in physics
for creatures like us,

00:50:20.531 --> 00:50:23.810
who coupled to the macroscopic
structure of the world,

00:50:23.810 --> 00:50:27.500
the fact that information
flows from the past

00:50:27.500 --> 00:50:30.348
and causal information
runs into the future

00:50:30.348 --> 00:50:33.555
is a fundamental, informative
feature of our experience.

00:50:33.555 --> 00:50:36.590
And one that frames every
aspect of our thoughtful

00:50:36.590 --> 00:50:38.120
engagement with the world.

00:50:38.120 --> 00:50:40.493
And when that's built
into all of our concepts.

00:50:42.430 --> 00:50:43.349
Now on the question

00:50:43.349 --> 00:50:47.210
of whether causes are
inside or outside the head,

00:50:47.210 --> 00:50:51.020
the right thing to say is
partly inside and partly outside

00:50:51.020 --> 00:50:52.683
for the reasons that I just gave.

00:50:53.870 --> 00:50:56.101
Are pre-scientific ideas about causation,

00:50:56.101 --> 00:50:59.480
aren't really, I think, articulated enough

00:50:59.480 --> 00:51:02.530
to single out one part
of this layered structure

00:51:02.530 --> 00:51:04.733
that leads from microscopic processes

00:51:04.733 --> 00:51:07.290
all the way up to the coupled interaction

00:51:07.290 --> 00:51:08.786
between agent environment,

00:51:08.786 --> 00:51:11.480
in which causal thinking has its home,

00:51:11.480 --> 00:51:13.433
as where the causes are.

00:51:13.433 --> 00:51:16.517
Scientifically there's no uniform usage.

00:51:16.517 --> 00:51:19.770
Physicists will speak of
space your temporal geometry

00:51:19.770 --> 00:51:21.700
is in coding causal structure,

00:51:21.700 --> 00:51:24.100
but when biologists talk
about causal structure,

00:51:24.100 --> 00:51:26.280
they mean something much higher

00:51:26.280 --> 00:51:28.614
in that stratigraphic hierarchy.

00:51:28.614 --> 00:51:31.151
When ordinary people talk about causation,

00:51:31.151 --> 00:51:34.573
they invoke ideas closely
associated with the phenomenology

00:51:34.573 --> 00:51:37.920
of pushing and pulling
something with a sort of quasi

00:51:37.920 --> 00:51:40.660
muscular notion of compulsion in mind.

00:51:40.660 --> 00:51:43.574
We can see how all of these ideas arise

00:51:43.574 --> 00:51:46.230
and how they relate to one another.

00:51:46.230 --> 00:51:48.850
And I don't really see that
there's a factual question

00:51:48.850 --> 00:51:50.891
to be settled about,
which of them captures

00:51:50.891 --> 00:51:52.492
the essence of causation

00:51:52.492 --> 00:51:55.678
or about which point at which
point in that hierarchy,

00:51:55.678 --> 00:51:58.663
we finally really have causes.

00:52:00.050 --> 00:52:03.260
Now notice that this kind
of account is different

00:52:03.260 --> 00:52:05.644
from what we might think
of as the prototypical

00:52:05.644 --> 00:52:08.589
philosophical analysis of a concept.

00:52:08.589 --> 00:52:10.831
And it's characteristic of the way

00:52:10.831 --> 00:52:13.340
in which we get a scientific account

00:52:15.114 --> 00:52:18.900
of one of those notions
that play a central role

00:52:18.900 --> 00:52:22.190
in mediating our practical
and epistemic interaction

00:52:22.190 --> 00:52:23.440
with the world.

00:52:23.440 --> 00:52:24.980
Other notions that are like this,

00:52:24.980 --> 00:52:29.278
I think are probability and
information to some extent.

00:52:29.278 --> 00:52:31.684
There's an external component.

00:52:31.684 --> 00:52:33.959
That is the infrastructure
in the environment

00:52:33.959 --> 00:52:37.924
that agents use as strategic
pathways to bring about ends.

00:52:37.924 --> 00:52:40.075
And there's also an internal component

00:52:40.075 --> 00:52:43.143
and the content of causal concepts

00:52:43.143 --> 00:52:46.680
understood partly in terms of their role

00:52:47.770 --> 00:52:50.623
in internal inferential networks.

00:52:50.623 --> 00:52:53.890
And a big part of understanding

00:52:53.890 --> 00:52:56.979
what causation is, is fitting
those pieces together.

00:52:56.979 --> 00:53:00.050
It saying how agents with
the kind of information

00:53:00.050 --> 00:53:00.883
that we have,

00:53:00.883 --> 00:53:03.758
and our practical
capacities for intervention

00:53:03.758 --> 00:53:06.769
in a world that is objectively
structured like ours,

00:53:06.769 --> 00:53:10.993
how those agents develop
and use causal ideas.

00:53:12.700 --> 00:53:14.797
So there are two
philosophically, I'm almost done.

00:53:14.797 --> 00:53:17.960
There are two philosophically
important aspects

00:53:17.960 --> 00:53:19.009
of the account.

00:53:19.009 --> 00:53:24.009
One is that it secures the
objectivity of causal relations,

00:53:24.470 --> 00:53:27.760
making apt objects for scientific study.

00:53:27.760 --> 00:53:30.370
And the second is that
it's a really good antidote

00:53:30.370 --> 00:53:32.380
to redefine causal relations

00:53:32.380 --> 00:53:36.238
as compulsive asymmetric
relations between local events

00:53:36.238 --> 00:53:39.801
built right into the
fundamental fabric of the world.

00:53:39.801 --> 00:53:41.397
There are causal pathways

00:53:41.397 --> 00:53:43.212
written into the fabric of the world,

00:53:43.212 --> 00:53:47.293
but those pathways are neither
asymmetric nor compulsive.

00:53:48.470 --> 00:53:50.846
We see, I think that that complete account

00:53:50.846 --> 00:53:52.770
of the metaphysics of causation

00:53:52.770 --> 00:53:55.076
is given by the facts about our world

00:53:55.076 --> 00:53:56.814
and about our position in it

00:53:56.814 --> 00:53:59.832
that support the acquisition
of news of causal concepts.

00:53:59.832 --> 00:54:03.438
And that account does not
treat causal relationships

00:54:03.438 --> 00:54:06.710
as asymmetric, pushy
relationship dependents

00:54:06.710 --> 00:54:10.093
that are written into the
fundamental fabric of the world.

00:54:10.941 --> 00:54:13.550
But it makes causal relations objective

00:54:13.550 --> 00:54:16.710
in all of the ways that
should matter to science,

00:54:16.710 --> 00:54:19.030
it makes perfect sense to think of science

00:54:19.030 --> 00:54:22.693
as devoted to, as Russell
put it, in his later years,

00:54:22.693 --> 00:54:26.233
discovering the causal
skeleton of the world.

00:54:27.296 --> 00:54:31.573
So I think of this kind of
account as a side-on view,

00:54:32.750 --> 00:54:35.263
the side-on view brings into focus,

00:54:35.263 --> 00:54:37.570
the assemblage, the side-on view

00:54:37.570 --> 00:54:39.660
because it takes a kind of side on look

00:54:39.660 --> 00:54:43.210
at the agent coupled to the world

00:54:43.210 --> 00:54:46.348
and the processes that
mediate its interaction.

00:54:46.348 --> 00:54:49.430
Now, the sidearm view brings into focus

00:54:49.430 --> 00:54:51.570
and assemblage of different structures,

00:54:51.570 --> 00:54:53.290
starting with geometry,

00:54:53.290 --> 00:54:56.740
becoming progressively more
articulated by the context

00:54:56.740 --> 00:54:59.860
and coming into sharp relief
in their distinctive role

00:54:59.860 --> 00:55:03.210
in practical reasoning
for situated agents.

00:55:03.210 --> 00:55:06.000
There are a number of
well-defined precise concepts

00:55:06.000 --> 00:55:07.950
as I said, that can be introduced

00:55:07.950 --> 00:55:10.020
to talk about structures
at different levels

00:55:10.020 --> 00:55:11.570
of this element.

00:55:11.570 --> 00:55:12.820
The reasons that I gave,

00:55:12.820 --> 00:55:15.640
I think no account of causal concepts

00:55:15.640 --> 00:55:17.960
that doesn't bring out
their distinctive role

00:55:17.960 --> 00:55:19.478
in practical reasoning

00:55:19.478 --> 00:55:21.500
and the way in which causal concepts

00:55:21.500 --> 00:55:24.332
are structured by that
rule can be complete.

00:55:24.332 --> 00:55:28.780
But we can't talk about causal
structure in the strip down,

00:55:28.780 --> 00:55:30.700
since that applies just at the level of

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:32.716
fundamental geometry.

00:55:32.716 --> 00:55:35.147
So that's the difference
between causal structure

00:55:35.147 --> 00:55:36.533
and causal concept.

00:55:37.830 --> 00:55:39.707
At that level, one thinks of space time,

00:55:39.707 --> 00:55:43.760
as in coding relations
of causal connectability,

00:55:43.760 --> 00:55:46.170
one can talk about also causal structure

00:55:46.170 --> 00:55:48.500
in a way that links it
to physical processes.

00:55:48.500 --> 00:55:50.648
As in the tradition that leads from

00:55:50.648 --> 00:55:53.350
Sam and through Phil Dowe.

00:55:53.350 --> 00:55:55.280
One can also talk about causal structure

00:55:55.280 --> 00:55:58.670
in a way that connects it to
the kinds of emergent pathways

00:55:58.670 --> 00:56:00.280
captured in DAGs,

00:56:00.280 --> 00:56:03.058
or one can talk about it in
a way that ties it to agency

00:56:03.058 --> 00:56:05.590
with all of the
articulation and influential

00:56:05.590 --> 00:56:06.822
and practical commitments

00:56:06.822 --> 00:56:08.710
that come from being embedded

00:56:08.710 --> 00:56:10.680
in a whole network of concepts.

00:56:10.680 --> 00:56:12.690
These notions don't compete.

00:56:12.690 --> 00:56:15.230
We shouldn't insist on a single notion

00:56:15.230 --> 00:56:17.283
that's gonna serve all of these purposes.

00:56:17.283 --> 00:56:19.531
Each one of them is well defined

00:56:19.531 --> 00:56:21.610
and we can see how causal emotions

00:56:21.610 --> 00:56:23.270
become increasingly structured.

00:56:23.270 --> 00:56:27.806
Increasingly articulated
as the setting is enriched.

00:56:27.806 --> 00:56:29.840
Is precisely because the notion,

00:56:29.840 --> 00:56:32.025
I think, spans all of these levels,

00:56:32.025 --> 00:56:33.702
from fundamental geometry

00:56:33.702 --> 00:56:36.313
up to the agent's strategic pathways,

00:56:36.313 --> 00:56:39.653
that it is so useful in
linking them together.

00:56:41.575 --> 00:56:43.480
As I said, I think something similar

00:56:43.480 --> 00:56:45.633
can be set of probability and information

00:56:45.633 --> 00:56:49.960
is precisely the level span
and character of these notions

00:56:49.960 --> 00:56:51.820
that makes them particularly well suited

00:56:51.820 --> 00:56:55.199
to bridging the divide between
purely physical description

00:56:55.199 --> 00:56:58.110
and the structured interface
that the mind brings

00:56:58.110 --> 00:57:00.683
to its understanding of nature.

00:57:03.110 --> 00:57:04.917
So the question of how.

00:57:04.917 --> 00:57:06.387
(words lost in cross talk)

00:57:06.387 --> 00:57:07.770
- [Priya] And this is really beautiful,

00:57:07.770 --> 00:57:10.040
the side-on view really powerful.

00:57:10.040 --> 00:57:11.400
I was just wondering,

00:57:11.400 --> 00:57:13.886
is there any ordering preferred ordering

00:57:13.886 --> 00:57:16.660
just like a hierarchy of ways of looking

00:57:16.660 --> 00:57:20.173
or are these just, is
it an ordering in these?

00:57:21.380 --> 00:57:24.090
- So there is a physical
ordering, so if one starts,

00:57:24.090 --> 00:57:25.010
so that's why I said,

00:57:25.010 --> 00:57:27.760
if one starts with physical
structure ordered by scale,

00:57:27.760 --> 00:57:30.223
then at the bottom level,
there's gonna be geometry.

00:57:30.223 --> 00:57:31.420
(words lost in cross talk)

00:57:31.420 --> 00:57:33.950
You can characterize
geometry independently

00:57:33.950 --> 00:57:34.797
of the matter content.

00:57:34.797 --> 00:57:37.238
And then when you enrich
it with the matter content,

00:57:37.238 --> 00:57:39.675
then you get like, cause
of process notions.

00:57:39.675 --> 00:57:43.190
And then when you add a
thermodynamic gradient,

00:57:43.190 --> 00:57:44.270
you get the asymmetry.

00:57:44.270 --> 00:57:46.471
So there's a sort of
natural, physical ordering.

00:57:46.471 --> 00:57:51.471
And as you add more to
the physical description

00:57:51.535 --> 00:57:52.750
of the system,

00:57:52.750 --> 00:57:55.940
you get increasingly enriched
notions of causation.

00:57:55.940 --> 00:57:58.076
So that's the way that I'm picturing it.

00:57:58.076 --> 00:58:01.280
- [Priya] So it's just
that the agents are really

00:58:04.272 --> 00:58:06.570
what I had a sort of question about

00:58:06.570 --> 00:58:07.672
whether these agents,

00:58:07.672 --> 00:58:11.811
you could have some
conception of the agents,

00:58:11.811 --> 00:58:15.449
even as you look at the connectability.

00:58:15.449 --> 00:58:20.449
So you may have some notion of agency

00:58:20.840 --> 00:58:23.915
which stands to be
verified and consolidated,

00:58:23.915 --> 00:58:27.670
but I was just curious about that.

00:58:27.670 --> 00:58:31.530
- Okay, so you're putting
your finger on something that

00:58:31.530 --> 00:58:32.363
it would take,

00:58:32.363 --> 00:58:35.920
I mentioned in passing and it
would take a good deal more

00:58:37.580 --> 00:58:40.940
to sort of articulate in a convincing way.

00:58:40.940 --> 00:58:42.760
But the way that I think about it

00:58:42.760 --> 00:58:45.100
is that it's actually the
thermodynamic gradient

00:58:45.100 --> 00:58:47.516
and the fact that the
thermodynamic gradient

00:58:47.516 --> 00:58:50.883
makes the macroscopic
structure of the world

00:58:50.883 --> 00:58:55.376
rich with information that
explains the emergence of agents

00:58:55.376 --> 00:58:56.890
in evolutionary terms.

00:58:56.890 --> 00:59:00.740
So if you think that what
agents are is by their nature,

00:59:00.740 --> 00:59:03.401
so to speak, but they're designed to do

00:59:03.401 --> 00:59:07.428
is be information gathering
and utilizing creatures

00:59:07.428 --> 00:59:11.110
then in a world where there
is no thermodynamic gradient,

00:59:11.110 --> 00:59:13.147
there's gonna be no agents.

00:59:13.147 --> 00:59:15.270
So agents were designed and selected

00:59:15.270 --> 00:59:18.710
because of the opportunities
for information gathering

00:59:18.710 --> 00:59:21.200
and use that's provided by
the thermodynamic gradient,

00:59:21.200 --> 00:59:22.530
they arise so to speak,

00:59:22.530 --> 00:59:24.213
to take advantage of those opportunities.

00:59:24.213 --> 00:59:29.213
And then that in its turn
explains why agents like us

00:59:31.590 --> 00:59:34.324
our senses are coupled to the macroscopic

00:59:34.324 --> 00:59:35.850
structure of the world.

00:59:35.850 --> 00:59:38.690
So sometimes people, I know
we're almost out of time,

00:59:38.690 --> 00:59:40.150
so I'll say this quickly,

00:59:40.150 --> 00:59:42.880
and maybe we can talk about it more,

00:59:42.880 --> 00:59:47.560
but I think what people
sometimes speak as though

00:59:47.560 --> 00:59:50.070
when you say that really
the asymmetry rises

00:59:50.070 --> 00:59:51.455
with the thermodynamic gradient

00:59:51.455 --> 00:59:55.330
and it's a macroscopic course graining

00:59:55.330 --> 00:59:58.035
imposed by the thermodynamic gradient

00:59:58.035 --> 00:59:59.670
that gives rise to the assymetry.

00:59:59.670 --> 01:00:02.750
They sometimes say, oh,
so it's just an artifact

01:00:02.750 --> 01:00:04.970
of the fact that we happen to have senses

01:00:04.970 --> 01:00:06.810
that parse the world,

01:00:06.810 --> 01:00:08.730
that see and of course, green way.

01:00:08.730 --> 01:00:10.029
And I think the right direction,

01:00:10.029 --> 01:00:12.570
you know, when you see
the whole assemblage

01:00:12.570 --> 01:00:13.962
in the way that I've sketched it,

01:00:13.962 --> 01:00:17.639
the right way to see the
direction of explanation is

01:00:17.639 --> 01:00:20.566
we see in macroscopic terms

01:00:20.566 --> 01:00:25.290
because that's where the opportunities

01:00:25.290 --> 01:00:28.623
for gathering and using information.

01:00:29.553 --> 01:00:32.408
- Thanks, I mean, we can
continue this offline.

01:00:32.408 --> 01:00:34.303
I get what you were at.

01:00:35.216 --> 01:00:37.000
- Good. I'm almost done.

01:00:37.000 --> 01:00:38.394
So I'll end quickly then.

01:00:38.394 --> 01:00:41.647
So the general idea, and
this is just a concluding

01:00:41.647 --> 01:00:43.470
to bring it back around.

01:00:43.470 --> 01:00:45.737
The general idea is that the question

01:00:45.737 --> 01:00:49.091
of how causation arises in
the structure of the cosmos

01:00:49.091 --> 01:00:52.530
has been alive one since Russell observed

01:00:52.530 --> 01:00:54.140
that it appears to have disappeared

01:00:54.140 --> 01:00:56.890
from the fundamental level
of physical description.

01:00:56.890 --> 01:01:00.120
So in 2003, Hartry Field called this,

01:01:00.120 --> 01:01:03.478
quote, the central problem in
the metaphysics of causation.

01:01:03.478 --> 01:01:06.350
And it's a question that's
animated with a good deal

01:01:06.350 --> 01:01:11.170
of recent discussion in philosophy.

01:01:11.170 --> 01:01:13.990
I transformed that question
into the question of

01:01:13.990 --> 01:01:15.355
how causation arises,

01:01:15.355 --> 01:01:17.405
the question of how causation arises

01:01:17.405 --> 01:01:19.959
into the question of how causal thinking

01:01:19.959 --> 01:01:23.743
and the structures that
support causal thinking arise,

01:01:23.743 --> 01:01:27.220
and then assembled the pieces
of what we have learned

01:01:27.220 --> 01:01:29.471
about causation the last 20 or 30 years

01:01:29.471 --> 01:01:32.650
into the outline of an
answer to those questions

01:01:32.650 --> 01:01:34.994
and suggested that once
those questions are answered,

01:01:34.994 --> 01:01:37.560
there's no specifically
metaphysical question

01:01:37.560 --> 01:01:39.960
about causation that remains.

01:01:39.960 --> 01:01:41.270
So this sort of naturalists,

01:01:41.270 --> 01:01:44.440
this is a kind of naturalistic pragmatism.

01:01:44.440 --> 01:01:47.160
That seems to me the only sensible away,

01:01:47.160 --> 01:01:50.670
only sensible way to approach
ontological questions,

01:01:50.670 --> 01:01:52.840
whether it's a question of what cause are,

01:01:52.840 --> 01:01:55.573
what causation is or what democracy is.

01:01:56.934 --> 01:01:58.249
Thank you.

01:01:58.249 --> 01:02:00.900
- Great, thank you so much Jenann

01:02:00.900 --> 01:02:04.250
for this really thought provoking and

01:02:05.750 --> 01:02:07.910
sort of exquisite explanation.

01:02:07.910 --> 01:02:09.360
I mean, there are obviously

01:02:09.360 --> 01:02:11.345
really deep foundational questions

01:02:11.345 --> 01:02:15.730
and it's really sort of
testimony to your clarity

01:02:15.730 --> 01:02:17.600
that you touched upon many of them.

01:02:17.600 --> 01:02:21.420
So I would like to a big round of applause

01:02:21.420 --> 01:02:25.810
and I see a lot of people
have the clap emoji on.

01:02:25.810 --> 01:02:29.409
So at least I wanted to
give you an auditory clap.

01:02:29.409 --> 01:02:30.910
Thank you so much.

01:02:30.910 --> 01:02:34.163
So I think we'll open
up for questions now.

01:02:35.010 --> 01:02:39.130
So I noticed there's a question
from Alexander (indistinct).

01:02:39.130 --> 01:02:40.780
So he says,

01:02:40.780 --> 01:02:44.890
I noticed that in the structure
at different levels slide

01:02:44.890 --> 01:02:48.520
that you showed one thing
that wasn't on the list

01:02:48.520 --> 01:02:51.419
was a statistical mechanical
probability distribution

01:02:51.419 --> 01:02:53.284
over microstates,

01:02:53.284 --> 01:02:56.330
this looks essential
to the Albert picture,

01:02:56.330 --> 01:03:00.315
but you don't seem specially
wedded to this precise account.

01:03:00.315 --> 01:03:03.210
So the question is to
what extent do you think

01:03:03.210 --> 01:03:06.060
introducing non-dynamic chance

01:03:06.060 --> 01:03:10.180
is essential for giving an
account of emergent causation?

01:03:10.180 --> 01:03:13.254
- I think it's essential and
actually what the probability

01:03:13.254 --> 01:03:14.436
postulate was meant to be

01:03:14.436 --> 01:03:17.028
this is called mechanical
probability distribution

01:03:17.028 --> 01:03:20.160
and is essential to the whole picture.

01:03:20.160 --> 01:03:21.903
So sorry if that wasn't clear.

01:03:24.250 --> 01:03:26.125
- Great. Thanks for that clarification.

01:03:26.125 --> 01:03:29.270
And we have another
question from Peter Morgan

01:03:29.270 --> 01:03:30.350
who is asking,

01:03:30.350 --> 01:03:33.056
are there other orderings then scale

01:03:33.056 --> 01:03:36.736
that we could have in
mind for a side-on view,

01:03:36.736 --> 01:03:40.933
systematic organization for example?

01:03:44.110 --> 01:03:45.630
- Yes, absolutely.

01:03:45.630 --> 01:03:49.530
And that would be natural
