01:04:18.080 --> 01:04:22.700
- Okay, so there are two
questions you might be asking

01:04:22.700 --> 01:04:26.340
one is whether quantum
mechanics plays any role

01:04:26.340 --> 01:04:28.020
in this story and the answer is no.

01:04:28.020 --> 01:04:30.700
So this whole sort of hierarchy

01:04:30.700 --> 01:04:34.130
is sort of erected on effectively.

01:04:34.130 --> 01:04:36.440
As I told the picture's erected on

01:04:36.440 --> 01:04:40.234
a kind of effectively
classical level of description.

01:04:40.234 --> 01:04:44.270
If you're asking, well, what
does one say about causation

01:04:44.270 --> 01:04:45.710
in quantum mechanics?

01:04:45.710 --> 01:04:47.500
Huge open question.

01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:48.333
I think,

01:04:51.360 --> 01:04:53.080
there are different ways
that one could answer it

01:04:53.080 --> 01:04:55.423
depending on how one disambiguates.

01:04:56.440 --> 01:04:58.120
The different notions of causation.

01:04:58.120 --> 01:04:58.953
So if you're asking,

01:04:58.953 --> 01:05:01.683
are there causal processes
in quantum mechanics?

01:05:01.683 --> 01:05:05.303
That is, chains of interactions,

01:05:06.310 --> 01:05:11.310
involving the exchange of
conserved quantity, the answer is

01:05:14.760 --> 01:05:17.960
not right now, not in space time,

01:05:17.960 --> 01:05:19.330
is the answer to that.

01:05:19.330 --> 01:05:21.370
So if you're asking,

01:05:21.370 --> 01:05:26.078
are there sort of interventionists notions

01:05:26.078 --> 01:05:29.240
of causation that are
applicable in quantum mechanics?

01:05:29.240 --> 01:05:30.170
The answer is yes.

01:05:30.170 --> 01:05:32.273
And in fact, some people have used

01:05:32.273 --> 01:05:35.830
interventionists notions to
argue that quantum mechanics

01:05:35.830 --> 01:05:39.320
almost by its nature
involves retro causality.

01:05:39.320 --> 01:05:40.860
So you take the quantum formulas

01:05:40.860 --> 01:05:43.594
and you ask yourself what
happens if I intervene here

01:05:43.594 --> 01:05:45.790
and you see causal relations under

01:05:45.790 --> 01:05:47.829
certain conditions running into the past.

01:05:47.829 --> 01:05:51.409
So big open question,
really interesting question.

01:05:51.409 --> 01:05:53.870
And I think there's a number
of different questions

01:05:53.870 --> 01:05:54.800
that one can ask,

01:05:54.800 --> 01:05:57.606
but it's not one that played any role

01:05:57.606 --> 01:06:00.163
in the story that I told.

01:06:02.240 --> 01:06:04.532
- So we have a question,

01:06:04.532 --> 01:06:07.651
sort of a clarification request from Paul.

01:06:07.651 --> 01:06:10.166
So is causation structure asymmetric

01:06:10.166 --> 01:06:12.141
at the microscopic level?

01:06:12.141 --> 01:06:13.786
If you're saying it is not,

01:06:13.786 --> 01:06:17.530
how could that be given
the low entropy state

01:06:17.530 --> 01:06:19.210
that we started from?

01:06:19.210 --> 01:06:22.570
- At the micro it
absolutely is asymmetric.

01:06:22.570 --> 01:06:24.607
So again, you know, there were some,

01:06:24.607 --> 01:06:27.230
I was sort of trying to
assemble the big pieces,

01:06:27.230 --> 01:06:29.670
so I didn't go into details
of retails little ones.

01:06:29.670 --> 01:06:32.639
And when I did it, wasn't clear
enough, but here's the idea.

01:06:32.639 --> 01:06:33.967
So you start the,

01:06:33.967 --> 01:06:37.400
I'm gonna use kind of the Albert accounts

01:06:38.570 --> 01:06:41.040
though you can make twiddles
to the Albert account

01:06:41.040 --> 01:06:43.590
if you object to the particular
form that he has of the past

01:06:43.590 --> 01:06:45.480
hypothesis as a probability possible,

01:06:45.480 --> 01:06:48.020
that just clear as to
say it in that setting,

01:06:48.020 --> 01:06:49.272
here's the idea.

01:06:49.272 --> 01:06:51.949
You assume low entropy boundary condition,

01:06:51.949 --> 01:06:54.810
you take the presence or
the able macro structure

01:06:54.810 --> 01:06:55.643
of the world.

01:06:57.230 --> 01:06:59.313
And you assume a probability possibility.

01:07:00.420 --> 01:07:05.420
And you ask what happens if I
intervene on a kind of online

01:07:05.658 --> 01:07:09.570
environment in the kind
of way that we think of

01:07:09.570 --> 01:07:11.584
normally when we're talking
about the sorts of manipulations

01:07:11.584 --> 01:07:14.919
that embedded agents like us can make?

01:07:14.919 --> 01:07:17.270
How do you answer that question

01:07:17.270 --> 01:07:19.340
in the statistical mechanical framework?

01:07:19.340 --> 01:07:22.020
You take the probability distribution,

01:07:22.020 --> 01:07:23.461
you evolve at first past

01:07:23.461 --> 01:07:25.530
to find out whether the intervention

01:07:25.530 --> 01:07:26.953
makes a difference to the past,

01:07:26.953 --> 01:07:28.770
and then you evolve it forward

01:07:28.770 --> 01:07:30.255
to find out whether the intervention

01:07:30.255 --> 01:07:31.904
makes a difference to the future.

01:07:31.904 --> 01:07:34.118
When you evolve it backwards,

01:07:34.118 --> 01:07:38.359
if your conditionalizing on a
low entropy state in the past,

01:07:38.359 --> 01:07:42.076
you will get the results
that it makes no difference

01:07:42.076 --> 01:07:46.480
to any event of which there
is a macroscopic record.

01:07:46.480 --> 01:07:49.150
And if you think about it
for like a minute and a half,

01:07:49.150 --> 01:07:52.516
you'll find out that includes
pretty much everything

01:07:52.516 --> 01:07:54.910
that we could form any
sort of belief about this,

01:07:54.910 --> 01:07:57.804
at least potentially a
macroscopic record of some kind

01:07:57.804 --> 01:08:00.400
about whether or not the event took place.

01:08:00.400 --> 01:08:02.496
If you do that in the forward
direction, you say again,

01:08:02.496 --> 01:08:05.629
you take the low entropy
boundary conditioning past,

01:08:05.629 --> 01:08:08.106
presence available
macrostate of the world,

01:08:08.106 --> 01:08:11.507
and you ask what happens if I
intervene on the environment?

01:08:11.507 --> 01:08:15.420
So I step in the sand, I built a house,

01:08:15.420 --> 01:08:17.100
I dig a ditch.

01:08:17.100 --> 01:08:20.748
And what you find when you evolve,

01:08:20.748 --> 01:08:22.940
the probability distribution forward

01:08:22.940 --> 01:08:26.390
is pretty much what common sense thinks of

01:08:26.390 --> 01:08:30.330
as the effects of those
kinds of interventions

01:08:30.330 --> 01:08:32.140
are the ones that are
assigned high probability

01:08:32.140 --> 01:08:33.873
by the probability distribution.

01:08:35.155 --> 01:08:38.380
So that's the way that you
get the causal asymmetry

01:08:38.380 --> 01:08:40.480
out of the statistical mechanical account.

01:08:41.520 --> 01:08:45.940
- So then Ken Winkler has
a question that actually,

01:08:45.940 --> 01:08:47.829
I also had the same question, which is,

01:08:47.829 --> 01:08:51.277
can you say a bit more about
your naturalistic pragmatism

01:08:51.277 --> 01:08:54.680
and your inclination to dispense
with more traditional forms

01:08:54.680 --> 01:08:56.350
of philosophical analysis,

01:08:56.350 --> 01:08:59.162
perhaps by illustrating
how it might be applied

01:08:59.162 --> 01:09:02.690
to one of the cases that you
mentioned as you concluded?

01:09:04.208 --> 01:09:07.269
- This is a problem with period
talk where you say too much.

01:09:07.269 --> 01:09:10.840
So that's a huge question,
but I'll say for example,

01:09:10.840 --> 01:09:11.900
with information.

01:09:11.900 --> 01:09:13.813
So wait, let me say a little bit.

01:09:15.130 --> 01:09:19.160
The kind of naturalistic
pragmatism is not a conceptual

01:09:19.160 --> 01:09:20.510
analysis of these notion.

01:09:20.510 --> 01:09:24.513
So what it does is it takes
it's target of explanation.

01:09:25.426 --> 01:09:30.243
Why would agents like us have
developed concepts like that?

01:09:31.507 --> 01:09:34.440
What are the external structures

01:09:34.440 --> 01:09:37.120
that support the application
of those concepts

01:09:37.120 --> 01:09:39.100
and how do those concepts feature in

01:09:39.100 --> 01:09:42.090
the internal economy of the agent?

01:09:42.090 --> 01:09:44.541
So think about something like information.

01:09:44.541 --> 01:09:47.040
This is a notion that's
been, you know, sort of,

01:09:47.040 --> 01:09:49.100
it gets used a lot in physics

01:09:49.100 --> 01:09:50.933
and it's one of these notions that's

01:09:50.933 --> 01:09:53.252
contested in the way that causation is.

01:09:53.252 --> 01:09:54.674
'Cause many people will say,

01:09:54.674 --> 01:09:56.640
look, you're applying causal notions

01:09:56.640 --> 01:09:58.518
to physical explanation,

01:09:58.518 --> 01:10:00.633
but the notion, oh sorry,

01:10:01.780 --> 01:10:03.845
the notion of information
to physical explanation,

01:10:03.845 --> 01:10:06.870
but information isn't something
that we can define directly

01:10:06.870 --> 01:10:09.520
in causal terms it's
intentionally laid in.

01:10:09.520 --> 01:10:11.720
It's got all of these associations

01:10:11.720 --> 01:10:14.170
that come from the use
of language and so on.

01:10:14.170 --> 01:10:17.060
And what I want to say
in a case like that is,

01:10:17.060 --> 01:10:20.170
well, just like the case of causation,

01:10:20.170 --> 01:10:23.310
we can start at the fundamental level

01:10:24.260 --> 01:10:25.590
of just physical description

01:10:25.590 --> 01:10:27.970
before we have the introduction of agents

01:10:27.970 --> 01:10:31.120
or anything like that with
notions of information that are

01:10:31.120 --> 01:10:33.960
stripped down and that we can introduce

01:10:33.960 --> 01:10:35.430
in those stripped down ways

01:10:35.430 --> 01:10:38.298
that play a role in low level description,

01:10:38.298 --> 01:10:41.413
making sure that we're not
importing associations.

01:10:41.413 --> 01:10:44.527
And then as we add matter and we add,

01:10:44.527 --> 01:10:47.560
you know ultimate and we add evolution,

01:10:47.560 --> 01:10:50.743
we add biology and we
add ultimately agents.

01:10:50.743 --> 01:10:54.250
So they're not just using
information to guide behavior,

01:10:54.250 --> 01:10:56.630
not only, okay, wait, let me back up.

01:10:56.630 --> 01:10:58.030
There's a crucial point.

01:10:58.030 --> 01:11:00.630
There's a crucial step in
the case of information

01:11:00.630 --> 01:11:03.398
that's worth pausing to dwell on.

01:11:03.398 --> 01:11:06.273
When we're starting to talk about

01:11:06.273 --> 01:11:09.200
systems that were evolutionarily designed,

01:11:09.200 --> 01:11:12.355
we do get more structure
in that can give rise to,

01:11:12.355 --> 01:11:15.030
that impose a little bit more articulation

01:11:15.030 --> 01:11:17.166
on the notion of information.

01:11:17.166 --> 01:11:20.230
So now we have slightly richer notion

01:11:20.230 --> 01:11:24.210
and then we start getting systems
that are using information

01:11:24.210 --> 01:11:25.560
in more and more sophisticated ways,

01:11:25.560 --> 01:11:28.945
ultimately agents that
are introducing concepts

01:11:28.945 --> 01:11:29.940
and inferences.

01:11:29.940 --> 01:11:32.040
And again, it gets more articulated

01:11:32.040 --> 01:11:33.277
until finally we have something

01:11:33.277 --> 01:11:38.277
that's rich enough to be applied
to linguistic interactions.

01:11:38.510 --> 01:11:42.330
So that's another very schematic sketch

01:11:42.330 --> 01:11:44.460
of the kind of thing that I have in mind.

01:11:44.460 --> 01:11:47.801
And at no point do you say,
this is what information is,

01:11:47.801 --> 01:11:49.960
information is purely physical,

01:11:49.960 --> 01:11:51.825
or information is purely mental

01:11:51.825 --> 01:11:54.090
is something that plays a role

01:11:54.090 --> 01:11:56.263
at different levels of that hierarchy.

01:12:01.270 --> 01:12:06.270
- Great, I think Peter Morgan comment

01:12:07.640 --> 01:12:08.960
so crudely put,

01:12:08.960 --> 01:12:13.130
does a site-on view say
that the past hypothesis

01:12:13.130 --> 01:12:16.209
could be true or false
is a global statement

01:12:16.209 --> 01:12:19.069
about all length or other scales,

01:12:19.069 --> 01:12:20.473
provided for example,

01:12:20.473 --> 01:12:24.950
all creatures like us start
from the same direction in time

01:12:24.950 --> 01:12:26.143
with no memory.

01:12:27.897 --> 01:12:30.070
- So I'm gonna read the question again.

01:12:30.070 --> 01:12:32.690
I'm not sure in the past hypothesis

01:12:32.690 --> 01:12:35.313
could be true or false
as a global statement.

01:12:36.721 --> 01:12:40.701
Yes, so the past hypothesis
though is a statement

01:12:40.701 --> 01:12:44.070
about the macro state of the world.

01:12:44.070 --> 01:12:47.728
So it says that the universe started

01:12:47.728 --> 01:12:51.204
in some microstate that has low entropy.

01:12:51.204 --> 01:12:56.204
That is a member of a
low entropy macrostate

01:12:56.447 --> 01:13:01.447
when the thermodynamic
core screening is applied.

01:13:01.900 --> 01:13:03.897
So it's by its nature
in a way it classifies

01:13:03.897 --> 01:13:05.795
the early microstate of the world

01:13:05.795 --> 01:13:10.630
by reference to the
thermodynamic core screening.

01:13:10.630 --> 01:13:11.920
Does that? Okay.

01:13:15.003 --> 01:13:16.430
- Peter does that?

01:13:16.430 --> 01:13:19.770
Yeah, I guess he was putting
back to Carlo's Talk,

01:13:19.770 --> 01:13:22.423
where the focus was on memory, right?

01:13:23.350 --> 01:13:24.183
- Right.

01:13:24.183 --> 01:13:26.696
So what I've been talking about

01:13:26.696 --> 01:13:29.761
as the capacity of the universe

01:13:29.761 --> 01:13:33.115
to bear information about its past state,

01:13:33.115 --> 01:13:35.370
that's what Carlo was talking about

01:13:35.370 --> 01:13:37.204
when he talked about memory.

01:13:37.204 --> 01:13:42.204
So, and that depends
entirely on the low entropy

01:13:43.970 --> 01:13:45.670
initial condition of the universe.

01:13:48.595 --> 01:13:53.595
- Just waiting to see if there
are any further questions,

01:13:58.150 --> 01:14:00.253
let's give people a minute.

01:14:01.870 --> 01:14:06.870
So I don't see any pressing
questions, but anyway,

01:14:07.326 --> 01:14:10.800
we could thank you so much Jenann.

01:14:10.800 --> 01:14:13.883
We can wrap up today and
if there are any questions,

01:14:15.040 --> 01:14:18.195
we can hold on to them
for our discussion session

01:14:18.195 --> 01:14:22.730
tomorrow afternoon at
the same time, 3:00 pm

01:14:22.730 --> 01:14:26.270
with Jim Woodward, as the
conversant and discussant

01:14:26.270 --> 01:14:28.200
with Jenann.

01:14:28.200 --> 01:14:30.736
So thank you so much
everyone for attending

01:14:30.736 --> 01:14:32.720
and a special thank you to Jenann

01:14:32.720 --> 01:14:34.478
for a wonderful, inspiring talk.

01:14:34.478 --> 01:14:38.155
- Thank you for coming and
